WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.320
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.670
This is such a wonderful audience

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:16.670
and it's really quite a
thrill and an honor to be here

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.530
to welcome Anderson Cooper

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:24.530
as our 2023 Spring Chubb fellow.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.260
My name is Mary Lui.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.950
I am the Head of College
for Timothy Dwight College,

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.105
and it is truly-

00:00:34.105 --> 00:00:37.623
(audience cheering)
Oh.

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.290
Thank you, TD!

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:44.578
It's always-

00:00:44.578 --> 00:00:46.630
(audience cheering)

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:47.640
(Mary laughs)

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.423
- [Man] I think you packed the house.

00:00:49.423 --> 00:00:52.426
- (laughing) We might have
tipped the scales a little bit.

00:00:52.426 --> 00:00:53.640
(audience laughing)

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.860
So before we begin today's conversation

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:56.880
with Anderson Cooper,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.150
I'm just gonna ask that all of
you turn off your cell phones

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.760
and refrain from taking any photographs

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.443
while the program is going.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.670
I would also like to start our program

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.380
with a land acknowledgement.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.030
Today's Chubb Fellowship event

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.000
sits on traditional
indigenous territories.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.610
This includes lands of the Mohegan,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.380
the Mohican, the Mashantucket
Pequot, Eastern Pequot,

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.140
Schaghticoke, Golden Hill Paugussett,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.730
Niantic, Quinnipiac,

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.280
and other Algonquian-speaking peoples.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.100
We honor and pay respect to their peoples

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.813
of past and present.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.930
As I said, my name is Mary Lui.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.000
I'm the head of College
of Timothy Dwight College.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.550
I'm a professor in the
department of History

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.890
and the program in American Studies.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.330
I also have the great honor

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:49.650
of being the custodian
of the Chubb Fellowship,

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.750
and I wanna give you a
little bit of information

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.510
and background about the
Chubb before we begin.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.360
Established in 1941,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.390
the Chubb Fellowship came
out of a large donation

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.240
for "education purposes",

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.880
made in 1936 by Hendon Chubb,

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.420
a graduate of the Yale class of 1895.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:10.680
Since its establishment,

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.480
the Chubb Fund has adhered to the goals

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.240
of providing encouragement
and aid to students

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.020
interested in government
and American public affairs.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.570
The fellowship initially aimed to foster,

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.400
among Yale undergraduates,

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.990
an interest in public service
and local and state affairs,

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:28.950
grew over the years

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.200
to include numerous distinguished visitors

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.720
in national and international affairs,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.740
as well as leaders in the world
of the arts and humanities.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.300
Since the 1940s,

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.100
the Chubb Fellowship lecture series

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.070
has inspired generations of Yale students

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.400
to undertake public service
and pursue leadership roles

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.460
in the hopes of creating
a better world today

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.200
and for posterity.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.290
The fellowship has hosted
four US presidents,

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.810
George H. W. Bush, Gerald Ford,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.360
Jimmy Carter, and Harry Truman.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:01.890
In recent years,

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.830
we have welcomed a wide range of national

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.090
and international leaders,

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.760
including our congressional
representative,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.770
the Honorable Rosa Deloro,

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.080
violin virtuoso, Hillary Hahn,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.860
highly decorated of Olympic
figure skater, Michelle Kwan,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.960
visual artist, Faith Ringold,

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.690
environmental activist, Bill McKibben,

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:25.020
Somali physician and
humanitarian, Dr. Hawa Abdi,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.850
and so many more.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:29.310
So with this quick
description of the history

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.350
and aims of the Chubb Fellowship,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.170
I'm deeply honored to have Anderson Cooper

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.570
join the impressive list of Chubb fellows

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:40.590
who have visited Timothy Dwight
College in Yale's campus.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.553
So welcome, Anderson.

00:03:42.553 --> 00:03:46.053
(Mary, audience clapping)

00:03:51.475 --> 00:03:55.230
And now I wanna welcome to
the stage, James, Jimmy Hatch,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.490
to introduce Anderson
Cooper fully and properly.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:00.961
Come on up.

00:04:00.961 --> 00:04:03.544
(all clapping)

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:08.663
- Thanks, Mary.

00:04:08.663 --> 00:04:10.890
Thanks, everybody, who clapped.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.710
I don't know why you would
do that, but whatever.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:15.060
I know everybody wants to hear Anderson,

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:15.930
so I'm gonna make this quick,

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.100
but I think it's important.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.080
I met Anderson when he interviewed me,

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:20.940
I think it was 2015,

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.740
and he made quite an impact on me.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:23.970
I'd seen him on television

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.980
and I'd seen him do some things

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.390
that, to me, were rather impressive

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:28.890
and I'll get to those in a second.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:30.440
First thing I wanna do is read,

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:32.870
so you get to know him...

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:35.910
(audience laughing)

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.590
See, this is not unlike Anderson.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.543
He fixed my tie once in an interview.

00:04:39.543 --> 00:04:42.870
(audience laughing)

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.712
Get it together, Mary. (laughing)

00:04:44.712 --> 00:04:46.560
(audience laughing)

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.530
So before his late mother passed,

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.287
he and she wrote this book,

00:04:52.287 --> 00:04:54.750
and I found this part
of it really beautiful

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.250
knowing Anderson a little bit.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.200
So I'm just gonna read her description.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.597
To quote Elizabeth Browning,

00:05:01.597 --> 00:05:03.120
"I love you in ways that are infinite

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.150
and as in eternity, have
no beginning or end.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.650
But are we alike?"

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:09.307
And again, this is his mother speaking.

00:05:09.307 --> 00:05:10.680
"Sometimes I think hardly at all.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.510
You are the living image of your father,

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:13.440
not only in appearance,

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.130
but in valuing the same
standards he held in high regard,

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.290
standards that are
sometimes hard to live by.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.320
Other times, yes, we are
indeed completely alike.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.300
We share sensitivity, tempered by poise

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.780
and the reserve to reveal only
what we wish to communicate.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.750
We have the gift of editing,
presenting ourselves

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.250
in different ways to different people.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:33.720
We are not gossips.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:34.890
We are worthy to be trusted

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.860
with secrets of family and friends.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.450
We have fun going to the movies together,

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:41.190
sharing a bag of popcorn.

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:44.160
We do not burden each
other with trivial troubles

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.553
or pull each other down
with unsupportive comments.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:51.000
We advise each other on pressing
matters from time to time.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.400
And you always give me a short, sensible,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.070
well-thought-out solution to any problem.

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.350
I try to respond in the same manner.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.630
We both spend a lot of
time organizing our lives,

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.270
secretly working out various
options and scenarios

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:04.500
in our heads.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:06.480
Luckily, yours are far more practical

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.460
and more likely to
become reality than mine,

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:09.690
which continue to be influenced

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:12.930
by the enchanted fairy
tales I read as a child.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:13.763
In spite of this,

00:06:13.763 --> 00:06:14.760
I treasure those fairy tales

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.170
for they are, in large part,
what spur my creativity.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:21.600
We share a restless spirit.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.190
In fact, we are never at rest.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:26.760
And rarely, if ever, will
anything satisfy Andy and his mom.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.710
Soon something beckons.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:31.470
It's there around the
corner, just out of sight.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:32.913
All we need to do is follow.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:36.450
All my life, I have
craved and long for love,

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:37.650
and I have not been deprived.

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:39.780
My cup has runneth over,

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:41.070
but it was never enough.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:44.460
It never got anywhere near
what the trouble really was.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:45.360
No mother, no father,

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.600
as my secret sister, Susan
Sontag, knew too well.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.940
Here, Andy, thankfully, we are not alike.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.490
Even though you lost your
father when you were 10,

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:55.800
you knew that there
wasn't a moment from birth

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.080
when you didn't have and know his support,

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.020
how fully completely he
loved you and Carter.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.270
Although only those years were given,

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:05.100
they were enough for him to pass on to you

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.830
the values that have made
you the person that you are,

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.557
my son, treasured each moment of my life."

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.790
I don't think you can get
much better than that.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.680
Now, as for me personally,

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:17.943
I remember watching,

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.240
I was in the SEAL Teams
and in the SEAL Teams,

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:22.950
people like Anderson, journalists,

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.000
are essentially the same

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.830
as any other enemy we have. (chuckles)

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.560
So I was rather-
(audience laughing)

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.780
I was rather harsh on journalists.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.040
But I remember watching.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:33.300
It was in the early nineties,

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:34.290
and I've discussed this

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.903
with one of Anderson's camera guys, Neil.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:41.670
They were in Haiti and there
were some troubles there.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:43.413
Often there are troubles in Haiti.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.290
But Anderson was standing
at an intersection.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:47.160
He was on camera.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:47.993
I was watching him,

00:07:47.993 --> 00:07:49.470
and I think I was actually
overseas somewhere

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:50.820
when I was watching it.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.040
And he's talking

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.070
and all of a sudden you can see
in the buildings behind him,

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.650
rocks and other projectiles hitting them.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.660
And it was getting ugly quick.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:04.350
And Anderson immediately
turned and grabbed this kid,

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.760
threw him under his arm

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.430
and started running
outta the intersection.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:09.880
And I thought to myself, man,

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.960
that's something you're
either born with or not.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.310
You can't train somebody to do that,

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:15.150
to be that thoughtful

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:17.310
in the middle of emergency
situation like that,

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.260
to grab someone who can't be protected

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.670
and get 'em outta there.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.280
So he became less of an enemy

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.470
at that point to me. (chuckles)

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.870
And then I was in New York

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:30.000
after Anderson and I had gotten
to be a little bit closer.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.880
I was in New York for another function,

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.590
and I went to the CNN Studios

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.060
and it happened to be the same time

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:38.280
that the Pulse Nightclub
shooting had gone down.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:40.470
So Anderson was in Orlando.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.870
And I was in the production room.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:45.240
It's really cool to see
how the sausage is made,

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:46.890
so to speak, how busy people are.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:48.480
And Anderson was down there

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.760
and he was reporting on the shooting.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:52.560
And then they went to a commercial.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.300
And during the commercial,

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.610
Anderson had a long piece of paper

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:58.080
and he was reading off names.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.210
And there was one of the
producers in the booth

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:04.110
was helping him so that he
could say the names properly.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.070
And some of 'em were tough

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:09.090
and it was so emotional
and Anderson was emotional.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:10.950
But his drive to be respectful

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.470
and to make sure that
everybody who lost their life

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:16.800
was acknowledged and done
so in a respectful way.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.120
And the way he did that,

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.013
in that short chunk of time,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.340
and earnestly showed me who he was.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:24.930
And then when he read their names,

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:26.763
he became, he's human.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:28.623
He's not a robot.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:31.503
And I was taken by that.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.490
The thing that is the most
important about Anderson to me,

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:36.930
and it's something that
is really important

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.090
that I've learned here
at Yale in my short time

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.890
and through my time in the military.

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:45.570
We are in a time when it's
easy to dehumanize one another.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.340
And Anderson interviews
a lot of different people

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.290
and is in a lot of different situations.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.720
And I've never ever seen him,

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:52.980
even though he might disagree,

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:55.680
I've never seen him
dehumanize another person.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.210
And I think that's a super
important thing right now.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:58.800
And I think it's an example for all of us.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.450
And with that, I'll shut up
and let 'em get after it.

00:10:02.759 --> 00:10:04.530
(audience clapping)

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:06.270
- I realize in my excitement

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.640
to just get going with
the conversation with you,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.410
I didn't properly introduce you, Jimmy,

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:11.880
and I really apologize.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:13.140
So I'm gonna say-
(Jimmy murmurs)

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:14.880
No, no, I'm gonna do it because you know,

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.770
Anderson won't forgive me if I don't.

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:19.710
So who you just heard from

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:21.540
is a wonderful member

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.700
of our Timothy Dwight College community.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:27.240
This is James, Jimmy
Hatch, third year student.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.010
He's part of the Eli Whitney Program.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:32.280
He's also the founder
of the Spikes K9 Fund,

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.940
which advocates for the US
working canine population.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.470
So he's done incredible work there

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:42.900
and cannot thank you
enough for all you do.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.900
So, okay, with that,

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:47.430
I think we can segue.
- Hello.

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:48.600
- Hello. (laughing)

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:49.980
- Reading the list of names of people

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:50.813
who've done this before,

00:10:50.813 --> 00:10:53.742
I feel like I've really
brought the gradient down.

00:10:53.742 --> 00:10:54.575
(audience laughing)

00:10:54.575 --> 00:10:56.874
I feel like you guys were
desperate or something, but-

00:10:56.874 --> 00:10:57.707
(audience laughing)

00:10:57.707 --> 00:10:58.590
I appreciate being here though.

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:01.230
And thank you all for coming.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.350
- Um, no...
(audience laughing)

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:05.640
We were very thoughtful.

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:06.473
There were many other people.

00:11:06.473 --> 00:11:08.340
- You shouldn't start
off with US presidents.

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:09.173
That's-
(audience laughing)

00:11:09.173 --> 00:11:11.430
It's a little intimidating,
I gotta say. (laughing)

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:12.810
(audience laughing)

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:13.680
- Oh, I don't know.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:14.520
- Especially, Truman.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:15.837
Like, wow, okay.

00:11:15.837 --> 00:11:17.100
Did he like sit here?

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.160
Was he here?
- No.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:19.440
- Okay.
- No. No.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:21.260
That would be the wrong timeline.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.443
- Yeah, I know.
- But that's okay.

00:11:23.443 --> 00:11:28.443
So I would say, as we were
joking before on our way here,

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:32.040
that for me preparing for
this conversation with you

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:35.070
was a little bit like cramming for an exam

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:39.060
where I was madly reading
and rereading works of yours.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:40.980
- Right, ChatGPT will take care of that.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:42.919
Don't worry.
(Mary and audience laughs)

00:11:42.919 --> 00:11:44.307
- Ah!
(Anderson laughs)

00:11:44.307 --> 00:11:46.560
The TD community knows
a little bit too much

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.683
about me and ChatGBT,
but that's another story.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.740
But in all seriousness,

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:55.560
looking at all the things
you have accomplished

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:59.160
and here you are with your
CNN badge right out here,

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.290
along with the publication

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.300
starting with "Dispatches from the Edge,"

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:07.568
and that was back in 2005, I wanna say.

00:12:07.568 --> 00:12:08.401
- Yeah.
- 2006?

00:12:08.401 --> 00:12:09.600
- Probably 2006, I'm thinking.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:10.890
Yeah, that's my first book I wrote.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.450
- Yeah and then "Vanderbilt:

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.510
The Rise and Fall of an
American Dynasty," 2021.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:18.270
- Yes.
- There's a lot in between.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.030
I don't quite understand
how you have the time

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:24.210
to do all of that while being on CNN,

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:25.950
while being on 60 Minutes

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:28.230
and then doing all the
things that you've done.

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:30.270
So I'm curious to hear

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:33.720
how you see your writing projects

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:34.650
that you've done along the way.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:35.483
If you don't mind.
- Sure.

00:12:35.483 --> 00:12:36.900
- I would actually like to start there.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:37.733
- Yeah.

00:12:37.733 --> 00:12:40.710
- And has there been a pathway,

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:42.890
a vision of what you would be, yeah.

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:45.000
- So yeah, so I've written,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.630
well three, I'm writing a fourth book now.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:50.250
My first book, "Dispatches From the Edge,"

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:51.990
was a book I'd been writing in my head

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:53.640
for probably 10 years.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.503
I wrote it in 2006.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.910
I got offered the opportunity to write it

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:02.283
during the Hurricane Katrina coverage,

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.310
which is when, I guess,

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:07.680
some people started to
notice me more a little bit.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:11.250
And so, actually, a kid I
went to high school with

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.260
who turned out to be a literary agent,

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:15.450
actually a kid I went to
like elementary school with

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:16.650
and have known my entire life,

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:17.850
who used to be in a heavy metal band

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:18.683
and I'd lost touch with him.

00:13:18.683 --> 00:13:21.120
He called me up and he turns
out he's a literary agent now.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.730
Like how did that happen?
(Mary laughs)

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:26.340
But yeah, he became my literary agent

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.647
and I wrote this book.

00:13:28.647 --> 00:13:30.930
And I'd been writing this book
in my head for a long time.

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:33.360
My brother, Carter, died by suicide

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:35.670
right before my senior year at Yale.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.500
So my senior year was kind of a blur here.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:39.120
I took a year off after college

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:42.030
and I became very interested in sort of,

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:43.710
and also my dad died when I was 10,

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:46.680
as my mom referenced in Jimmy's reading.

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:48.780
So I was always very
interested in sort of survival

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:52.020
and how, why two people
growing up in a house,

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.720
why does one person survive
and the other person doesn't,

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:58.980
the same kind of, we were
the same kind of people.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:00.063
What happened?

00:14:01.037 --> 00:14:03.720
And that always expanded
to sort of societies.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.393
I was interested in societies
that had fallen apart,

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:10.620
whether it's through war
or political situations

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:12.150
or whatever it may be.

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:15.390
And so, as a child, I
used to read a lot about,

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:18.720
I was fascinated by like Zaire,

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:21.723
which was at that time,
run by President Mobutu.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:24.180
So I would read a lot about that.

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.340
I was fascinated with like
British colonial wars.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:28.290
I was like, what the
hell was that all about?

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.990
Like these British people marching along,

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:32.070
attacking the Zulus.

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:36.780
And so, I was fascinated in
sort of places on the planet

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:37.860
that I wanted to go to.

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:41.943
And I came up with this
idea after college to,

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:44.580
ultimately, I ended up having a friend

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:45.630
make a fake press pass.

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:46.463
I borrowed a camera.

00:14:46.463 --> 00:14:48.093
I just started going into wars.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:53.010
But I was very much driven by this idea

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:54.810
of loss and survival.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:58.560
And so, even if I didn't really
plan on becoming a reporter,

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.270
I just, I wanted to go places

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.400
where the language of loss was spoken

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.350
and where people don't talk about grief

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:05.880
and loss here very much.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.680
And I certainly didn't.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:09.150
I found it very hard to talk about,

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:11.640
but I wanted to immerse myself in places

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:13.200
where people were suffering

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.780
and where people did speak about this

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:17.190
and I wanted to understand it.

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:20.673
And I found, when I got to some places,

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:24.030
I found an odd

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:26.790
ability to operate in these environments.

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:30.540
And I felt, in fact, easier in some ways,

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:32.940
not easier, but in some ways,

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.790
it was comfortable for me
to be in some environments

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:38.013
moreso than like being back home.

00:15:39.630 --> 00:15:42.210
So yeah, so that book was
really a book about death

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:43.800
and loss and survival.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:47.400
And so, it was sort of set
against the backdrop of 2005,

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:48.630
which had Hurricane Katrina

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:51.480
and the tsunami and all sorts of stories.

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:53.910
There was a lot of stories
I covered that year

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:57.540
and they all centered
around death and loss.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.070
So it was sort of the twin stories

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:00.585
of what happened to my brother

00:16:00.585 --> 00:16:05.585
and also all these places
I was along the way, yeah.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:07.560
And then I wrote a book with my mom.

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:10.080
Like I didn't write again
for like 10 years later,

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:12.753
10 more years or so, 'cause
I had nothing to say.

00:16:12.753 --> 00:16:15.330
And then my mom turned 91

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:17.310
and she sent me this
really kind of interesting,

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.560
odd, cool email, as she often did.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:23.517
And it was all about how she still felt 17

00:16:23.517 --> 00:16:25.080
and something her grandmother

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:26.640
had said to her when she was 17.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:30.930
Anyway, I decided we should
have a conversation for a year

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:33.240
'cause like when you have a parent,

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:34.800
often you just know them as your parent.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:37.950
You don't really know them
as a person or as a friend.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:41.490
And so, I knew my mom
extraordinarily well,

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:42.540
but there were still all these things

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:44.070
I kind of didn't understand about her.

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:46.380
And there's a lot I
don't know about my dad

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:49.140
and I thought, my mom's
91, I don't want her to die

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:52.260
and there would be anything
left unsaid between us

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:53.730
and I don't want there
to be any questions.

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:56.970
So if we had a conscious
conversation for a year.

00:16:56.970 --> 00:16:59.364
I sound like Gwyneth Paltrow,
like conscious uncoupling.

00:16:59.364 --> 00:17:01.170
(audience laughing)

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:03.180
We had a conscious
conversation for a year,

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:07.570
mostly over email because
I was traveling and stuff

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:09.840
where she would ask me questions,

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:11.370
I would ask her questions

00:17:11.370 --> 00:17:13.170
and we'd just go back and forth

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:15.660
and it was this amazing conversation

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:17.160
that I didn't intend to write as a book.

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:19.230
But about halfway through,
I was like, you know what?

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:21.840
Actually I think we could
kind of make this into a book.

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:24.750
And yeah, so that was that.

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:27.540
And then the Vanderbilt was
this book I wrote two years ago.

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:30.000
It was the first time I had a co-writer,

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.913
a historian, Katherine Howe,
who did most of the research.

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:36.300
And I didn't really know anything

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:37.440
about the Vanderbilt family.

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:39.330
I mean, my mom was Gloria Vanderbilt,

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:42.900
but she always thought of
herself as like a changeling.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:45.300
She thought she was sort of
an imposter in that family

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:46.590
and wasn't really part of that family

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:49.710
based on some crazy stuff
that happened to her as a kid.

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:52.650
And so, I grew up not really
knowing anything about them an

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.480
and I consciously didn't
really want to know

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:55.533
anything about them.

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:58.620
You know, when I got to
Yale, everyone was like,

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:00.630
oh, are you staying in
the Vanderbilt room?

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:02.550
And I was like, A, that's
not really a thing.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:05.310
And B, I wouldn't even if I could,

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:07.560
'cause I really want
nothing to do with them.

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:10.350
But then once I had kids,
I suddenly realized,

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:12.330
well, what am I gonna tell my kids

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:14.790
about this kind of weird thing
that happened in the past

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:16.590
that I'm related to apparently.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:19.680
And so, yeah, so I started doing research

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:21.480
and decided to write that book.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.137
- You know, it's funny until you said,

00:18:23.137 --> 00:18:25.110
"I had nothing to say for 10 years,"

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.690
it almost, I think again, when you do all,

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:29.580
you read everything all at the same time,

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:32.040
it feels like there hasn't been that gap

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:34.620
because in the first
book, the Dispatches book-

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:38.070
- I'm basically regurgitating
the same stuff, yeah.

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:39.600
I'm just waiting for
enough people to die off,

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.580
they don't realize-
(audience laughing)

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:44.730
Oh, it's the same book again. (chuckles)

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:47.640
- No, I don't think so.
(all laughing)

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:50.040
But I think looking at Dispatch,

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.260
what was really incredible,

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.120
was hearing your voice

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:57.480
and talking about the things in your life

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:59.933
that had been really with you and sort of-

00:18:59.933 --> 00:19:00.766
- Yeah.

00:19:00.766 --> 00:19:01.599
- But you hadn't really explored.

00:19:01.599 --> 00:19:02.432
- Yeah.

00:19:02.432 --> 00:19:05.520
- While taking us through the
insane year, that was 2005,

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:07.950
beginning with the 2004 tsunami.

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:09.120
- Yeah.
- So you're reading,

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.700
you're going back and forth
between your coverage,

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:13.950
dealing with so much death and destruction

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:17.610
and really feeling that
incredible responsibility

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:19.260
to every single person you're meeting.

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:20.760
- Right.
- To tell the story,

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:22.260
do more than bear witness.

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:25.200
I mean, you describe it so powerfully

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:27.840
as people are hoping you're
gonna make a difference,

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.870
by them talking to you.
- Yeah.

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:31.800
- While at the same time,

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:33.570
you're talking through your own efforts

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.060
to really talk about
the pain and suffering

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:37.410
you've personally gone through.

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:38.243
- Right.

00:19:38.243 --> 00:19:39.900
- So then you go from that to, I think,

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:41.133
the book with your,

00:19:43.084 --> 00:19:45.060
the conscious conversation
with your mother.

00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:46.497
It seems as though,

00:19:46.497 --> 00:19:48.540
and you never stopped
having that conversation,

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:49.560
I guess, is what I'm saying.

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:50.550
- Yeah, I mean,

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:53.280
yeah, those are all things I
still think about all the time.

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:57.330
I mean, I think for anybody
who lost a parent in this room

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:58.593
early on as a child,

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:04.980
anybody, if you lose a parent early on,

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:07.080
it changes the trajectory of your life.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.600
And I certainly wasn't
able to talk about it,

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:11.580
the death my dad, it was so overwhelming,

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:13.083
it was so stunning to me.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:15.783
And even now,

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:18.570
my voice shakes a little
bit when I think about it.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:21.210
And so, I like talking about it now,

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:23.310
but that was something
I just didn't speak of.

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:27.120
And so, going to places where
people did speak about loss

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:29.520
because they were in the midst of a loss,

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:31.530
they had just experienced
the loss of a child

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:32.670
or a loss of their family

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:37.383
or an entire village wiped
out or whatever it was,

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:40.710
I couldn't help but,

00:20:40.710 --> 00:20:42.930
I mean, I probably could
have just gone to therapy

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:44.220
and that would've probably

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.830
made a little more sense early on.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:51.830
But it was an extraordinary, I mean,

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:55.890
both as something that
I cared very much about,

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:58.630
bearing witness to what
is happening in the world

00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:02.730
and bearing witness to
the suffering of others

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:04.323
and the pain that others feel.

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:08.370
And also, yeah, I mean,
I couldn't help but feel

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:09.963
a commonality in some sense.

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:13.980
- Yeah, it seemed as one is working

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:17.220
our way through that book,
we land in New Orleans

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:19.950
in terms of Hurricane Katrina.

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:21.630
You're telling us again and again,

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:24.000
how shocking the whole
experience was for you

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.730
in terms of it being here
in the United States.

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:28.290
So I'm curious, prior to that,

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:30.360
was most of your career out in the world?

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:33.030
- Yeah, most everything
I did was overseas.

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:36.750
I really, I felt like there's
a lot of reporters in America

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:40.107
and like I'm like the guy
with a fake press pass

00:21:40.107 --> 00:21:41.040
and a camera.

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:42.780
Why am I gonna try to compete

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:44.940
with somebody at the
White House reporting?

00:21:44.940 --> 00:21:46.860
So yeah, my first story,

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:49.290
I snuck into Myanmar, Burma,

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:50.310
I hooked up with some students

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:51.810
fighting the Burmese government

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:53.643
and I shot a story about them.

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:59.190
I then ended up enrolling
in school in Hanoi

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:01.620
in the last six months of the US embargo

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:02.700
'cause I wanted to be someplace

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:03.720
where there weren't many Americans

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:06.450
'cause I thought that'd be
an interesting experience.

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:08.070
So there were very small
number of Americans

00:22:08.070 --> 00:22:09.420
in Hanoi at the time.

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:11.283
Like, I think maybe 20 or so.

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:14.520
And yeah, so I studied
Vietnamese for a while

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:16.050
and I shot five stories

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:18.250
and then I still didn't get a job

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.840
but I was airing these things
on a thing called Channel One,

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:23.940
which was a show seen in
about half the high schools

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:27.060
and middle schools in America
in the early nineties.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:28.590
If you know, you know and if you don't,

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:29.850
like you think it's ridiculous,

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:34.850
but it had this huge audience
of kids and teachers.

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:37.170
Nobody knew about it,

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:42.170
but I was a fact checker
there for like six months.

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:43.560
Then that's when I came up with the idea

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:45.810
of just going to wars
and that's what I did.

00:22:45.810 --> 00:22:48.150
And so, they would like air the stuff,

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:49.260
but they still didn't hire me.

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:52.500
And then, I'd left high school early

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:53.430
and I'd ridden in a truck

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:55.110
across Sub-Saharan Africa for six months.

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:58.680
So I was still sort of
familiar with Eastern Africa.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:22:59.910
And so, I pitched,

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:02.520
okay, I'm gonna go to 10 countries

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:03.420
over the next three months

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:04.710
and I'm gonna shoot 10 stories

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:05.543
and these are the stories

00:23:05.543 --> 00:23:06.870
I think I'm gonna be able to shoot.

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:09.360
And the first one was Somalia

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:12.210
in the early days of the
famine in August of '92

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:14.850
before the US got involved,
before "Black Hawk Down,"

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:16.020
if you've seen that movie.

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:17.670
It was about a year before that,

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:19.070
or eight months before that.

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:22.953
And yeah, and Somalia,

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:26.070
it had a big impact on
the Channel One audience.

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:27.990
A lot of people started
raising money for it and stuff.

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:29.940
And then they hired me full-time

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:32.640
as like their war person,
foreign correspondent.

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:35.730
So yeah, Bosnia and the
Civil War, Sarajevo,

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:37.680
briefly in Rwanda during the genocide,

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:38.820
South Africa, a lot,

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:41.880
for the run up to and the years before

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:43.280
and the election of Mandela.

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:46.583
Soviet Georgia, wherever
there was fighting,

00:23:46.583 --> 00:23:48.780
I mean, I would sort of go, Columbia,

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:52.140
Haiti for when the US
invaded after Raoul Cedras.

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:53.820
I was very interested in conflict

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:56.790
and I wanted to see,

00:23:56.790 --> 00:23:59.730
I wanted to experience and document

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:01.440
what people were going through.

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.150
- Yeah, for you, is there a big difference

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:07.080
between documenting something
like an environmental disaster

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:10.203
like a hurricane or a tsunami versus war?

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:13.830
- I mean, logistically,
there's some differences

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:16.260
in terms of, obviously,
safety and security.

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:17.643
There are differences,

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:19.920
which by the way, when I started,

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:23.160
nobody cared about my safety
or security other than me.

00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:24.960
So like, it wasn't like there was,

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:26.730
I didn't have like a
news network behind me,

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:30.720
so I was just out there by
myself doing really stupid stuff.

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:33.450
So I'm not encouraging
other people to do that

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:35.220
because there was a kid named Dan Eldon

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:36.660
who was doing the exact same thing I was

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:40.263
and he was hacked to
death by a mob in Somalia,

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:42.033
and others.

00:24:44.984 --> 00:24:46.173
But no, I think,

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:48.990
I mean, obviously,
everything is different.

00:24:48.990 --> 00:24:51.120
I try not to compare one thing to another

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:52.320
'cause I don't think there should be

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.540
like a sliding scale of sorrow.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.570
And I think you need to see
everything with fresh eyes.

00:24:57.570 --> 00:24:58.710
You need to see everything

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:01.440
as if it's the first time you're seeing it

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:03.630
because if you're so jaded,

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:04.530
at a certain point,

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:06.870
it's very easy to like start to compare

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:07.770
one thing to another.

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:10.342
Like, oh, it's not as
bad as this place was,

00:25:10.342 --> 00:25:11.520
you know, 10 years ago.

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:13.110
And if you start to do that,

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:15.480
you're not seeing it as you should see it.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:18.630
And that's a real risk that can happen.

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:21.250
I found myself in Rwanda

00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:24.930
right after the elections with Mandela.

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:26.190
I went to Rwanda

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:27.840
and it's a country I'd
been in a number of times

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:31.230
as a teenager, in happier times.

00:25:31.230 --> 00:25:33.900
And I was with the RPF,

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:35.310
the Rwanda Patriotic Front,

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:39.420
and went to some villages where
people had been slaughtered.

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:41.610
And I took a photograph

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:43.950
and by this point, I'd been
doing this for about three years

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:46.770
and I took a photograph
with my own personal camera

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:49.590
of five people who were
laying on the side of the road

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:52.320
who had decomposed on the side of a road.

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:56.760
And I was very interested in that moment

00:25:56.760 --> 00:26:01.170
in how the skin on this person's hand

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:03.120
had peeled off like a glove.

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:04.500
And I took a photograph of it

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:05.700
and there was another reporter,

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:08.940
a photojournalist who I'd linked up with

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:10.020
who were sort of traveling

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:12.090
'cause I had access to a vehicle

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:13.710
and he hitched a ride with us.

00:26:13.710 --> 00:26:15.360
He took a photograph of me doing that

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:16.590
and he sent it to me later.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:19.023
And he said, this is for
when you become famous.

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:23.970
And he said something else to me.

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:27.333
And what he was saying to me was,

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.700
you've gone too far.

00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:34.200
And I actually have that picture

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:36.030
up on my bulletin board at work

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:40.110
'cause it was a real, for me,

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:41.730
it was a realization that

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:44.293
I was no longer seeing those five people,

00:26:44.293 --> 00:26:45.900
I believe they were family.

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.000
I was no longer seeing them as a daughter

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:52.560
and a mother and a father
and probably, a son.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:53.700
I was interested in how

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:55.170
the skin was peeling off somebody's hand.

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:59.130
And so I stopped for quite a while

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:00.720
doing that sort of work

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:03.900
because I didn't want to forget,

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:06.150
like, I didn't want to see it like that.

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:08.520
I wanted to see it and be horrified by it

00:27:08.520 --> 00:27:13.323
and not interested in the sort
of the things that happen.

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:17.370
So yeah, I think that's an
important thing to keep in mind.

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:19.890
For me, that's a really
important thing, to not,

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:22.710
you run across reporters sometimes

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:23.790
who are very sort of jaded

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:24.870
and they've been around the world

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:26.070
and they've been doing it for years

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:29.100
and you're not doing justice to the people

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:30.180
you're there to report on,

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:31.233
if you can't,

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:33.750
if you're not horrified by things

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:35.613
and if you're not stunned by things.

00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:40.501
At least for me, I think, I can't do that.

00:27:40.501 --> 00:27:45.243
- Yeah, and you talk a lot about trying to

00:27:45.243 --> 00:27:49.968
keep that line and make
sure that your doing

00:27:49.968 --> 00:27:53.643
your due dilligence as
a reporter in terms of

00:27:53.643 --> 00:27:56.550
Forcing us to see the things
that we might not wanna see,

00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:58.110
but yet at the same time,

00:27:58.110 --> 00:28:02.190
I think as Jimmy so eloquently
said, never dehumanizing.

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:03.990
So it's not easy to do

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:06.390
and I really applaud you for doing that.

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:07.223
And I'm just curious,

00:28:07.223 --> 00:28:10.290
how do you manage to feel like

00:28:10.290 --> 00:28:11.990
you never lose sight of that line?

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:15.817
- I don't know.

00:28:15.817 --> 00:28:16.650
I mean, I hope I don't.

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:19.140
I mean, I try to be very conscious of it.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:20.733
I'm very interested in,

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:23.610
I'm sort of awkward around human beings.

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:25.770
I'm very shy and introverted,

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:27.660
but when I'm working,

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:31.470
I'm very engaged with somebody.

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:34.500
And it's sort of the only
time I can really engage

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:36.600
and really focus on somebody.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:39.552
And not the only time, I
mean, I'm not that weird.

00:28:39.552 --> 00:28:42.360
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:43.833
But I do think it's,

00:28:45.150 --> 00:28:45.983
yeah, I don't know.

00:28:45.983 --> 00:28:47.070
I don't really know how
to answer that question.

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:49.290
I mean, I think it's
important to not lose sight

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:52.290
of the fact that these
are all human beings

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:53.760
involved in things.

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:55.230
When we're covering politicians

00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:58.263
who are saying awful,
stupid, ridiculous things,

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:00.510
it's interesting,

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:03.030
it's easy to not think of
somebody as a human being.

00:29:03.030 --> 00:29:05.340
It's easy to just think of them as,

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:08.010
in Rwanda during, in the
run up to the genocide,

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:09.720
on the Rwandan radio,

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:11.370
they would call Tutsi's cockroaches.

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:13.320
And the whole idea is to dehumanize people

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:15.600
so that you can then take the next step

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:16.470
and do things to them.

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:19.140
So even people you don't like,

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:22.230
I think it's important to view
them as flawed human beings

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:24.660
and try to see, walk in their shoes

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:25.800
and see where they're coming from

00:29:25.800 --> 00:29:27.990
and understand why they're
making the arguments

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:28.823
they're making,

00:29:28.823 --> 00:29:30.390
what forces are at play in their world

00:29:30.390 --> 00:29:31.833
and in their work life.

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:36.513
But just on the day to
day coverage of things,

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:42.420
there was an active shooter
today in Louisville.

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:43.470
You know, I have a policy,

00:29:43.470 --> 00:29:45.840
I don't show active shooters,

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:47.220
I don't name them.

00:29:47.220 --> 00:29:49.620
History has recorded these people's names.

00:29:49.620 --> 00:29:51.300
And I don't think anybody in this room

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:53.040
can name somebody who died at Columbine.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:54.000
Maybe somebody could,

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:58.500
but you could probably name
the killers at Columbine

00:29:58.500 --> 00:30:00.240
much more than you can
name any of the people

00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:01.770
who were killed.

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:03.000
And I think that goes

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:05.190
for many of the active
shooting situations.

00:30:05.190 --> 00:30:08.820
So yeah, I just, I have a policy.

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:10.260
I don't show their pictures.

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:11.820
I report on them.

00:30:11.820 --> 00:30:14.040
I report on motivations.

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:16.380
I report on whatever
needs to be reported on,

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:20.850
but I try to always focus
on naming the people

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:21.683
who have been killed,

00:30:21.683 --> 00:30:23.460
naming those whose lives were lost.

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:24.750
And that's, I mean,

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:26.640
it's a small thing in the
grand scale of things,

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:27.873
but it does sort of,

00:30:29.610 --> 00:30:31.680
it is a guidepost for the way

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:34.353
I view a lot of things I report on.

00:30:38.220 --> 00:30:39.783
- You're clearly very busy.

00:30:40.741 --> 00:30:42.420
There's no lack of news in the world

00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:44.673
and today's shooting is
only one of many things

00:30:44.673 --> 00:30:46.080
that keep happening.

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:47.310
It must be really difficult

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:50.100
to be able to make the
decision of what gets aired,

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:54.750
what gets put on in
the time that you have.

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:56.673
- Yeah, I mean there's a certain,

00:30:57.570 --> 00:31:02.570
because I'm not motivated
by ideology and coverage,

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:05.640
so in some ways it's not that difficult

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:08.670
because things which are happening,

00:31:08.670 --> 00:31:12.153
my focus is what has
happened today or what is,

00:31:14.910 --> 00:31:16.140
what's in the news today

00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:19.170
or what's an important story?

00:31:19.170 --> 00:31:21.060
And some of it is tempered

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:23.190
by where do we have correspondence,

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:24.960
where are our people,

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:28.140
what stories do we have ready to go?

00:31:28.140 --> 00:31:29.340
What guests can we get?

00:31:29.340 --> 00:31:31.530
So some of the coverage is based on that.

00:31:31.530 --> 00:31:34.593
But you know, for me, it's not,

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:40.610
for me, it's based on what's
happened today usually

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:43.440
often takes precedence.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:45.780
But if it's obviously an ongoing story,

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:48.150
it's what the latest thing on that is,

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:51.330
rather than, if you're
ideologically motivated

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:52.530
and you have a channel

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:57.530
which is geared toward
spreading election lies,

00:31:58.020 --> 00:32:00.510
then your coverage can be based on,

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:01.980
it doesn't need to be based on anything

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:03.420
that's happened on that particular day.

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:05.070
It can be just driving toward that.

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:07.560
And that doesn't interest me.

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:11.613
- Have you ever had to really
push for airtime of something?

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:13.313
- Yeah, certainly.

00:32:13.313 --> 00:32:14.190
Look, it's always a discussion.

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:15.720
I have an executive producer
who I've worked with

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:18.120
for 20-something years at CNN.

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:19.110
He and I have been together

00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:20.910
in a lot of really dangerous places

00:32:20.910 --> 00:32:24.477
and he puts up with me.

00:32:24.477 --> 00:32:26.700
And so, I'm lucky to work with him.

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:28.560
And so, we know each other very well,

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:30.120
but he and I have,

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:32.310
I mean, it's an ongoing
discussion all day long.

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:34.680
We have a conference call in the morning.

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:37.470
All throughout the day,
we're figuring out,

00:32:37.470 --> 00:32:40.440
okay, everything we think
we're gonna do in the morning

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:41.940
that we think will be on the evening show.

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:44.700
I have a show that's
on from eight to nine.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:49.590
A lot of, 70% of that might
not make it on by the time.

00:32:49.590 --> 00:32:54.590
And the show we have ready
to go at 7:59 and 59 seconds,

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:56.730
the second before we go on air,

00:32:56.730 --> 00:32:58.917
that can all be tossed
out the moment we go on

00:32:58.917 --> 00:33:00.420
and I learn something else happened.

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:03.986
So the story in Tennessee
on the lawmakers,

00:33:03.986 --> 00:33:06.210
the three lawmakers who were voted,

00:33:06.210 --> 00:33:08.133
two of them were expelled.

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:13.410
You know, that started
breaking just 20 minutes

00:33:13.410 --> 00:33:16.650
or so before the eight o'clock show began.

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:20.310
And that was not what we had planned to do

00:33:20.310 --> 00:33:21.180
most of the show on,

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:22.080
but we ended up, I think,

00:33:22.080 --> 00:33:24.570
doing the first 30 minutes just live.

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:26.460
We had cameras in the hallway.

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:28.620
We had interviews with
one of the lawmakers

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:31.710
just after he was voted out.

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:34.590
We were following the
vote as it was going on.

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:36.930
We were able to pull in
interesting guests on it.

00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:41.160
So it's a combination of
factors that kind of go into it.

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:42.810
- So you're taking the day off today?

00:33:42.810 --> 00:33:44.130
- I am, yes.

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:47.223
Yes, it's a rare time that
I take the day off, yeah.

00:33:48.450 --> 00:33:50.640
Yes, it's sort of like
in the Charlie Brown

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:52.710
like in the Charlie Brown spelling bee,

00:33:52.710 --> 00:33:53.760
when you spell a word wrong,

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:55.170
it's like, (playfully murmuring).

00:33:55.170 --> 00:33:57.450
And then they just go like (lips puckers),

00:33:57.450 --> 00:33:58.440
you just disappear.
(Mary laughs)

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:03.000
So I feel like if I disappear
too long from the screen

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:04.350
that someone else will just replace me

00:34:04.350 --> 00:34:05.993
who's much smarter and better, so.

00:34:05.993 --> 00:34:06.826
(audience laughing)

00:34:06.826 --> 00:34:08.460
- So for one day, it's okay.
- One day, it's okay.

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:09.540
- One day, it's okay.

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:10.373
- Yes, yeah.
- Right.

00:34:10.373 --> 00:34:11.520
- So you won't have to
disappear at eight o'clock

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:12.480
to go do your show?

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:13.890
- We'll see, we'll see what happens.

00:34:13.890 --> 00:34:15.450
Lemme see what time it is now.

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:17.970
Oh, we got plenty of time.
(Mary laughs)

00:34:17.970 --> 00:34:19.680
- So yeah, I did wanna-

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:24.303
- I could, like honestly,
worst case scenario, it's 4:42.

00:34:25.222 --> 00:34:26.310
Like if I needed to,

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:29.430
I could do a show from here
probably at eight if I had to.

00:34:29.430 --> 00:34:31.202
- You could do it from dinner.

00:34:31.202 --> 00:34:32.160
- (chuckles) Well, yeah.

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:34.080
I'd probably have to
go off to a side thing.

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:38.640
But yeah, we've done
shows in some weird areas.

00:34:38.640 --> 00:34:40.490
- Okay, we will keep that in mind.

00:34:40.490 --> 00:34:41.700
- Okay.

00:34:41.700 --> 00:34:43.770
- What's the biggest
change for you as an anchor

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:46.620
versus being out there
traveling the world and-

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:47.580
- You know, the nice,

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:49.780
I mean, it's such a
ridiculous term, anchor.

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:58.260
I started out, as I said, just overseas

00:34:58.260 --> 00:35:00.750
and so for the first,
certainly three years,

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:02.910
I was just this person
with a small camera,

00:35:02.910 --> 00:35:04.320
I started getting like one producer

00:35:04.320 --> 00:35:05.580
or two producers from Channel One.

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:08.370
And we would all travel
together and we'd all shoot,

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:09.870
but it didn't seem to be going anywhere.

00:35:09.870 --> 00:35:11.940
It wasn't like, I didn't
think, I wasn't planning,

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:15.780
like this is my pathway to
finally getting that anchor job

00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:16.770
I've always dreamed of.

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:19.380
To me, the vision of an
anchor was like Kent Brockman

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:20.213
on The Simpsons.
(Mary laughs)

00:35:20.213 --> 00:35:23.130
So that I wasn't really aiming for that.

00:35:23.130 --> 00:35:26.080
And he is sort of an accurate
representation of some folks.

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:31.803
Then literally,

00:35:33.390 --> 00:35:37.066
Newsweek, there used to be a
magazine called Newsweek that-

00:35:37.066 --> 00:35:37.899
(audience laughing)

00:35:37.899 --> 00:35:38.880
Did an article about Channel One

00:35:38.880 --> 00:35:41.850
and they wrote about some
of the stuff I had done

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:43.230
'cause it was like this weird kid

00:35:43.230 --> 00:35:44.580
who shows up in these places

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:45.930
and he shoots these stories

00:35:47.947 --> 00:35:50.940
and ABC read that and asked for a tape

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:51.780
and I thought they were kidding.

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:54.270
I thought it was like a joke
that somebody was calling.

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:56.790
And anyway, I learned it wasn't.

00:35:56.790 --> 00:35:57.623
I put together a tape.

00:35:57.623 --> 00:35:59.340
And so, they hired me as a correspondent,

00:35:59.340 --> 00:36:01.047
which was insane 'cause I was 27.

00:36:01.047 --> 00:36:05.073
And in those days, they didn't
hire anybody in that age.

00:36:06.632 --> 00:36:08.823
But I never really wanted to,

00:36:08.823 --> 00:36:11.190
like anchoring just seemed

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:12.780
not like something I was
pretty interested in.

00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:14.400
But I started anchoring on the evening

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:15.963
on a overnight newscast.

00:36:17.070 --> 00:36:19.410
But the nice thing about CNN is you still,

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:20.760
they still want you out in the field.

00:36:20.760 --> 00:36:22.620
They still want you going to,

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:25.650
anchoring just gives you a
little bit more the ability

00:36:25.650 --> 00:36:27.150
to frankly choose where you go.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:29.550
So my day is much more
determined by myself

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:30.720
than it would be if I,

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:32.850
if I was a correspondent
waking up every day,

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:34.290
I might get sent someplace.

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:36.060
And I knew one day, I wanted to have kids

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:38.130
and I wanted to be able to
at least have some agency

00:36:38.130 --> 00:36:39.570
over my schedule.

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:41.640
But it's nice because you can,

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:44.490
your question earlier about
can you argue about where,

00:36:44.490 --> 00:36:47.100
you know, I can insist on going.

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:50.280
I did a whole week in DRC Congo,

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:51.420
which used to be Zaire,

00:36:51.420 --> 00:36:52.740
which I was obsessed with as a kid

00:36:52.740 --> 00:36:54.900
and I'm still fascinated by DRC Congo.

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:56.340
So I go there a lot

00:36:56.340 --> 00:36:58.410
and I did a whole week
of shows from there,

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:01.770
which was not necessarily where

00:37:01.770 --> 00:37:05.220
a news network is probably gonna
want to send you for a week

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:08.310
'cause it's very expensive
and difficult to work there.

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:10.650
But they knew it was an important story

00:37:10.650 --> 00:37:11.820
that I was wanting to do

00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:14.130
and I was doing something
story about sexual violence

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:17.520
on Eastern Congo and
they supported me on it.

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:20.760
So you're able to get stuff on the air

00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:21.870
that you might otherwise not,

00:37:21.870 --> 00:37:24.390
if you have, maybe if you're an anchor

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:25.590
'cause you have your own show.

00:37:25.590 --> 00:37:27.450
You can kind of determine that.

00:37:27.450 --> 00:37:29.340
- So I think I'm starting
to see a little bit

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:31.140
of how you're able to write books

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:33.480
'cause you're able to control
your time a little bit more,

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:34.920
in that sense.

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:38.250
- I also, I mean, until I've
had kids, I've had no life.

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:39.150
So like, I don't know,

00:37:39.150 --> 00:37:42.360
like you may think like I've
had some like glamorous fun.

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:44.250
Like this is all I've been doing.

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:47.790
Like I haven't been out there

00:37:47.790 --> 00:37:50.460
like enjoying myself like in you know,

00:37:50.460 --> 00:37:52.320
my entire twenties,

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:53.970
all my friends were like having dinners

00:37:53.970 --> 00:37:55.650
and going out to clubs and stuff

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:59.400
and I lost all my friends
'cause I was always away

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:04.110
and they like, I'd
constantly cancel any meetup

00:38:04.110 --> 00:38:05.700
or dinner or whatever I had with 'em

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:07.620
because if something would
happen, then I would just be off.

00:38:07.620 --> 00:38:09.507
And so, after a while people are like,

00:38:09.507 --> 00:38:11.130
oh, why even call this guy?

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:12.920
So it made it much easier for me-

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:13.950
- So you're that friend?
- I'm that friend.

00:38:13.950 --> 00:38:15.780
So it made it much easier
just focus on work.

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.610
And so, I've really,

00:38:17.610 --> 00:38:19.050
and I've loved it 'cause the work has been

00:38:19.050 --> 00:38:22.050
the most exciting thing
I could possibly imagine.

00:38:22.050 --> 00:38:24.060
Everything else pales in comparison.

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:27.870
I mean, there's nothing more
extraordinary in my mind than,

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:30.240
you know, something happens in the world

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:33.480
and I can get on a plane

00:38:33.480 --> 00:38:36.270
and I can actually go to wherever.

00:38:36.270 --> 00:38:37.650
I can go to Ukraine

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:41.190
and within a 24-hour period

00:38:41.190 --> 00:38:44.220
or however long it takes to get there,

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:46.830
I can be in a tank moving
toward a front line

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:49.890
with Ukrainian soldiers or I can be,

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:51.360
and that's an extraordinary thing,

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:55.290
to be able to drop into
other people's lives

00:38:55.290 --> 00:38:56.610
and in the most,

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:58.710
and it's not just visiting
other people's lives.

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:02.280
It's dropping in, in people's
lives in the most intense,

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:05.970
sometimes horrific but or
exciting or whatever it is,

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:08.970
most important moment
of their life, often.

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:13.020
Or often where their
life is under a threat.

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:15.420
And to be able to tell their story

00:39:15.420 --> 00:39:18.060
and allowed to tell their story by them

00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:22.530
and to kind of be able
to try to do justice

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:25.570
to the sights and the
sounds and the smells of it

00:39:27.194 --> 00:39:29.970
and bring that, and send
that and package that

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:33.630
and write it in a way that is accurate,

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:36.810
to me, why wouldn't I
do that all the time?

00:39:36.810 --> 00:39:37.643
It's incredible.

00:39:37.643 --> 00:39:40.080
I cannot believe I get to do it.

00:39:40.080 --> 00:39:43.260
- So you describe that
and you've written a book

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:45.690
that is more of a history work.

00:39:45.690 --> 00:39:46.800
- Yes.
- Vanderbilt,

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:50.410
which the research process can't be

00:39:51.462 --> 00:39:54.300
as exciting and glamorous
as what you just described.

00:39:54.300 --> 00:39:57.039
I assume it's a lot of pouring over.

00:39:57.039 --> 00:39:57.872
That's my my world.

00:39:57.872 --> 00:40:00.390
- Well, as I said, this is
the first book I co-wrote,

00:40:00.390 --> 00:40:04.650
so Katherine Howe, who is a
great historian and writer,

00:40:04.650 --> 00:40:07.140
she did most of the legwork on it,

00:40:07.140 --> 00:40:09.183
so I cannot claim to have,

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:14.703
I benefited from the arduous
hours that she put in.

00:40:15.960 --> 00:40:19.720
But I certainly read
extensively history books

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:23.250
but for me, what was interesting about

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:24.660
trying to write a history book

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:27.120
and particularly on the Vanderbilts is,

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:30.660
look, there have been
Pulitzer prize winning books

00:40:30.660 --> 00:40:32.220
about Commodore Vanderbilt,

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:35.700
who was my great, great, great or great,

00:40:35.700 --> 00:40:38.100
three or four greats, grandfather,

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:40.950
who was not a nice person.

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:42.930
I mean he was probably,

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:45.090
he had a self-described mania for money.

00:40:45.090 --> 00:40:47.190
He very well could have been a psychopath.

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:49.410
There're a lot more psychopaths out there

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:51.060
than I think we all realize

00:40:51.060 --> 00:40:54.363
in the world of business
and maybe media as well.

00:40:55.728 --> 00:41:00.480
But I wanted to, to me
what was interesting is not

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:03.390
to try to write a linear history

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:06.300
'cause there's no way I
would write something as good

00:41:06.300 --> 00:41:07.860
as has already been written out there,

00:41:07.860 --> 00:41:11.550
but was to kind of drop in moments

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:14.020
in these historical figures lives

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:18.690
and kind of see them as humans

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:21.930
and see them sort of how the money,

00:41:21.930 --> 00:41:23.940
the ripple effects of the,

00:41:23.940 --> 00:41:26.250
you have this patriarch of a family

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:29.460
who is obsessed with money
and has a mania for money.

00:41:29.460 --> 00:41:33.030
And from the age of 11 and builds,

00:41:33.030 --> 00:41:37.052
by the year Commodore
Vanderbilt died in 1877,

00:41:37.052 --> 00:41:39.285
one out of every $15,

00:41:39.285 --> 00:41:42.450
one outta every $20 in
circulation belonged to him.

00:41:42.450 --> 00:41:44.970
So he died with a hundred million dollars,

00:41:44.970 --> 00:41:46.800
which today is not,

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:50.610
people, you think, well,
Bezos's boat cost 500 million,

00:41:50.610 --> 00:41:52.080
but a hundred million dollars back then,

00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:54.810
I mean, it was an extraordinary figure.

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:56.940
He had more money than
the US Treasury had.

00:41:56.940 --> 00:41:59.700
And his son doubled that
fortune in eight years,

00:41:59.700 --> 00:42:01.710
which is incredible.

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:02.940
And what's even more incredible

00:42:02.940 --> 00:42:04.470
is that the third and fourth generation

00:42:04.470 --> 00:42:06.030
went on a spending spree,

00:42:06.030 --> 00:42:07.770
the likes of which you cannot believe.

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:10.650
And unlike the Astors, who
I'm writing a book about now,

00:42:10.650 --> 00:42:11.880
they didn't reinvest it

00:42:11.880 --> 00:42:14.100
in like the land that became New York.

00:42:14.100 --> 00:42:16.950
They built these ginormous palaces

00:42:16.950 --> 00:42:18.924
and gave Vanderbilt Hall.

00:42:18.924 --> 00:42:20.730
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:23.130
They built these enormous palaces

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:26.220
that were torn down within 60 years

00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:28.020
because they were so bloated

00:42:28.020 --> 00:42:32.250
and needed 60 servants to run them

00:42:32.250 --> 00:42:34.920
and tax laws changed and
inheritance taxes changed.

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:36.948
And no one wanted to live in these houses.

00:42:36.948 --> 00:42:39.210
And they were sold off for scrap.

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:41.733
And to me that's
fascinating, the arc of that.

00:42:42.750 --> 00:42:45.660
You know, it's always
interesting 'cause you know,

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:46.920
I come from this family

00:42:46.920 --> 00:42:50.280
and for my entire life everybody assumed

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:53.400
that I was like the Scion
of the Vanderbilt family

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:56.580
and would be inheriting great
wealth from the Vanderbilts,

00:42:56.580 --> 00:42:58.350
like as if there was some sort of

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:00.030
giant Vanderbilt slush fund

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:03.750
that I was just about to be
immersed in at age of 21.

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:05.250
And both my parents,

00:43:05.250 --> 00:43:09.300
like my dad took me to see the
Commodore Vanderbilt statue,

00:43:09.300 --> 00:43:11.160
which is outside Grand
Central Station in New York

00:43:11.160 --> 00:43:14.220
as he founded Grand Central Station

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:19.220
and he owned the 16 acres of
midtown Manhattan at the time.

00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:20.960
Don't know what happened to that.

00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:21.793
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:43:21.793 --> 00:43:23.430
But my dad took me,

00:43:23.430 --> 00:43:25.410
my dad who grew up very
poor Mississippi on a farm,

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:27.630
and took me to see this
statue when was like,

00:43:27.630 --> 00:43:29.967
I don't know, 12 or something or maybe 10.

00:43:29.967 --> 00:43:31.080
And to explain to me

00:43:31.080 --> 00:43:33.810
that this is like my
great-great great grandfather.

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:37.710
And the only thing I
came away understanding

00:43:37.710 --> 00:43:39.990
is that all grandparents turned
into statues when they died.

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:41.490
So I must have been-
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:43:41.490 --> 00:43:42.993
Like six actually at the time.

00:43:43.950 --> 00:43:48.030
But what he was showing me
was to then to let me know

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:49.050
that as I grew older,

00:43:49.050 --> 00:43:50.910
I would hear more about this family

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:52.560
that my mom had belonged to.

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:55.230
But that to not buy into it,

00:43:55.230 --> 00:43:57.137
to not believe that there was some

00:43:57.137 --> 00:43:59.430
like a pot of gold waiting
for me 'cause there wasn't,

00:43:59.430 --> 00:44:00.570
there was no trust fund,

00:44:00.570 --> 00:44:01.920
there was no nothing like that.

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:04.260
And I'm very lucky
that, that was the case.

00:44:04.260 --> 00:44:06.390
'Cause you look at the
history of the Vanderbilts,

00:44:06.390 --> 00:44:09.153
all of whom inherited great fortunes,

00:44:10.500 --> 00:44:12.780
a lot of 'em didn't do
much with their lives.

00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:13.890
They spent a lot of money.

00:44:13.890 --> 00:44:15.450
In fact, one of them said that

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:18.063
inheriting money equals like,

00:44:19.230 --> 00:44:20.160
I've forgotten his quote,

00:44:20.160 --> 00:44:22.500
but it's like, it's as bad as cocaine

00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:25.080
because like you think
it's gonna last forever

00:44:25.080 --> 00:44:27.720
and it feels great and you do nothing.

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:31.140
And that's really, to me,

00:44:31.140 --> 00:44:33.600
sort of why I wanted to
write this book, for my kids,

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:35.610
'cause I don't want them to feel

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:37.650
like that there's some pot
of gold waiting for them

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:39.480
because there's not.

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:41.040
- It's just a giant cautionary tale.

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:42.930
- It's a giant cautionary tale, yes.

00:44:42.930 --> 00:44:45.120
I think, I'm fascinated by this whole,

00:44:45.120 --> 00:44:48.060
like all these people who we all look to,

00:44:48.060 --> 00:44:50.130
people, not the People Magazine,

00:44:50.130 --> 00:44:52.140
you look at people on the
cover of People Magazine

00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:54.060
and all these people we read about

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:55.650
and look at on social media

00:44:55.650 --> 00:44:57.810
and we think, wow, it's incredible.

00:44:57.810 --> 00:44:59.640
I view them as like deeply warped

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:01.350
and in need of serious help.

00:45:01.350 --> 00:45:02.370
Like-
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:45:02.370 --> 00:45:04.800
I mean, I'm fascinated that we all are

00:45:04.800 --> 00:45:06.900
like brought up to look at these people

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:09.510
and think like, oh my God, if only,

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:11.220
and like when you actually
look at their lives,

00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:13.710
the misery of, Commodore Vanderbilt,

00:45:13.710 --> 00:45:15.810
he sent his wife to an insane asylum

00:45:15.810 --> 00:45:17.678
so he could (indistinct) the babysitter.

00:45:17.678 --> 00:45:20.340
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:45:20.340 --> 00:45:21.427
It's true.

00:45:21.427 --> 00:45:23.340
(audience laughing)

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:24.840
- So I was gonna say,

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:28.260
with so many people that
you could have chosen to,

00:45:28.260 --> 00:45:29.343
I mean obviously,

00:45:31.247 --> 00:45:33.780
your statue ancestor
has to be in the story

00:45:33.780 --> 00:45:35.861
'cause he's the star of the whole thing.

00:45:35.861 --> 00:45:36.694
- Yes.

00:45:36.694 --> 00:45:38.280
- But once you get past him,

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:41.160
there's so many people you
could have lingered on.

00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:43.323
