- Yeah.

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:26.040
Yeah, so basically, I
mean it's, I don't know.

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:30.403
- It puts like the Jersey Shore
Housewives show to whatever.

00:46:30.403 --> 00:46:31.860
- Yes, yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:46:31.860 --> 00:46:34.230
- Yeah, there was this epic feud.

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:35.670
It's too long and involved to go into.

00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:37.470
I can't even, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:39.170
- We'll have to talk about it off camera.

00:46:39.170 --> 00:46:41.010
- Okay.

00:46:41.010 --> 00:46:42.300
- So much there.

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:43.590
But no, I'm really-

00:46:43.590 --> 00:46:45.450
- I can tell you're a
historian 'cause this-

00:46:45.450 --> 00:46:48.180
- I know, I was just like
loving every single moment.

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:50.220
You have such a beautiful way of,

00:46:50.220 --> 00:46:51.420
you and Katherine Howe,

00:46:51.420 --> 00:46:53.760
have such a beautiful way
of rendering the past.

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:56.730
And so much of this history
I'm very familiar with,

00:46:56.730 --> 00:46:59.130
but I really, I felt like I
was there at these crazy balls.

00:46:59.130 --> 00:47:00.390
- Yeah, it's nice

00:47:00.390 --> 00:47:03.210
'cause there was a lot
of first person accounts

00:47:03.210 --> 00:47:04.043
with the Vanderbilts.

00:47:04.043 --> 00:47:05.030
With the Astros, actually not so much.

00:47:05.030 --> 00:47:07.202
So it's actually, it's tricky.

00:47:07.202 --> 00:47:09.720
But yeah, the Astros actually
destroy their records

00:47:09.720 --> 00:47:11.778
pretty much every generation,
which is interesting.

00:47:11.778 --> 00:47:14.310
- Hmm.

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:15.480
- Yeah, so.

00:47:15.480 --> 00:47:18.998
- Yep and then poor Consuelo
being locked into her room.

00:47:18.998 --> 00:47:19.890
- Yes.
- Before her wedding.

00:47:19.890 --> 00:47:20.910
- Yeah.
- All those things.

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:22.380
- Right, 'cause Consuelo Vanderbilt

00:47:22.380 --> 00:47:24.180
was like married off to,

00:47:24.180 --> 00:47:26.520
the Vanderbilts, they had
money, but they had no,

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:28.350
they didn't have entree
in New York society.

00:47:28.350 --> 00:47:31.530
So back then, they had so much money that,

00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:33.390
that seemed important to them to,

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:35.940
to like get into New York society.

00:47:35.940 --> 00:47:39.250
And one of the ways was to
start marrying off people

00:47:40.848 --> 00:47:41.681
to British nobility.

00:47:41.681 --> 00:47:43.470
So they would get a British title

00:47:43.470 --> 00:47:44.670
and all the British nobility

00:47:44.670 --> 00:47:46.410
were all like counts of no account,

00:47:46.410 --> 00:47:47.700
as my grandmother used to say.

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:48.533
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:47:48.533 --> 00:47:50.790
Meaning they had the title
but they had no money.

00:47:50.790 --> 00:47:52.260
So it was this perfect match

00:47:52.260 --> 00:47:53.410
of like Nouveau riches,

00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:57.990
ridiculously wealthy, tacky New Yorkers

00:47:57.990 --> 00:48:00.360
marrying the Duke of Marlboro.

00:48:00.360 --> 00:48:03.547
And they get married and
misery ensues for generations.

00:48:03.547 --> 00:48:05.460
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:48:05.460 --> 00:48:06.480
It's true.

00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:08.260
They're all miserable, yeah.

00:48:08.260 --> 00:48:10.140
- Yeah, they do not look happy.

00:48:10.140 --> 00:48:11.250
- No, they're miserable.
- No, no.

00:48:11.250 --> 00:48:12.083
- Yeah.

00:48:13.140 --> 00:48:17.793
- Well, so, when is the
Astro book gonna come out?

00:48:18.856 --> 00:48:19.980
- I think in September.

00:48:19.980 --> 00:48:21.400
- Oh wow, so you're almost there.

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:22.860
- Yeah, yeah.

00:48:22.860 --> 00:48:25.860
Yes, I'm doing edits right now.

00:48:25.860 --> 00:48:26.693
- Okay.
- Yeah.

00:48:26.693 --> 00:48:28.620
- Well, maybe we'll have
you back when it's over.

00:48:28.620 --> 00:48:29.453
- Okay. Sure.

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:32.400
- But you have another
project that's going

00:48:32.400 --> 00:48:35.940
and that's been really
different, the podcast.

00:48:35.940 --> 00:48:37.870
- Yes, that's what the world
needs, another podcast.

00:48:37.870 --> 00:48:40.530
Yeah, yeah.
(audience laughing)

00:48:40.530 --> 00:48:42.900
- Maybe not, but your
podcast is really special.

00:48:42.900 --> 00:48:44.670
- My podcast is special.
- Your podcast is special.

00:48:44.670 --> 00:48:45.503
- That is true.

00:48:45.503 --> 00:48:46.560
Thank you for saying that.

00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:49.110
Yeah. (laughing)

00:48:49.110 --> 00:48:49.943
So yeah.

00:48:51.975 --> 00:48:54.510
I had no interest in doing a podcast.

00:48:54.510 --> 00:48:57.540
I did not think the world
needs another podcast.

00:48:57.540 --> 00:48:59.520
And yet, here I am.

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:01.290
I did an eight season,

00:49:01.290 --> 00:49:03.990
eight episode podcast
about grief and loss.

00:49:03.990 --> 00:49:08.430
And I found, my mom died three years ago

00:49:08.430 --> 00:49:10.713
and yeah, 2019,

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:15.000
and almost four years ago.

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:17.160
And while going through her stuff,

00:49:17.160 --> 00:49:18.330
which is this process,

00:49:18.330 --> 00:49:21.003
everybody who has lost
somebody goes through,

00:49:21.960 --> 00:49:24.150
I found it incredibly
lonely and isolating.

00:49:24.150 --> 00:49:26.430
And in going through her stuff,

00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:29.640
my mom was like a very fancy hoarder.

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:30.990
There was a lot of stuff

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:33.810
and it was also my father's stuff

00:49:33.810 --> 00:49:34.800
and my brother's stuff

00:49:34.800 --> 00:49:36.960
'cause all of that had
just been packed away

00:49:36.960 --> 00:49:38.790
and nobody had ever gone through it.

00:49:38.790 --> 00:49:41.310
So I'm the last surviving member

00:49:41.310 --> 00:49:44.284
of the little family that I was born into.

00:49:44.284 --> 00:49:49.284
And so, I was going through
a storage room full of boxes

00:49:50.970 --> 00:49:54.060
and it was like the storage room in,

00:49:54.060 --> 00:49:55.650
I dunno if you remember the closing scene

00:49:55.650 --> 00:49:56.850
of "Raiders of the Lost Ark,"

00:49:56.850 --> 00:49:58.230
where there's like all just,

00:49:58.230 --> 00:50:01.410
the ark is stored in
this warehouse of boxes.

00:50:01.410 --> 00:50:03.630
And I like that nobody below the age of 25

00:50:03.630 --> 00:50:04.463
is nodding their head

00:50:04.463 --> 00:50:05.443
'cause they haven't even seen that movie.

00:50:05.443 --> 00:50:07.410
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:08.243
But...

00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:12.060
So I just found it incredibly isolating

00:50:12.060 --> 00:50:14.850
and I started, I'm a
big, I like this book,

00:50:14.850 --> 00:50:16.680
Viktor Frankl's, "Man's
Search for Meaning,"

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:19.620
and one of the things
Viktor Frankl writes about

00:50:19.620 --> 00:50:23.142
is he's a psychologist
who was in Auschwitz.

00:50:23.142 --> 00:50:26.730
He talks about narrating experiences,

00:50:26.730 --> 00:50:28.860
horrible experiences in his head

00:50:28.860 --> 00:50:30.870
as a way of going through them.

00:50:30.870 --> 00:50:33.510
And so, he narrates them as he would

00:50:33.510 --> 00:50:37.737
sort of a professor narrating
a clinical situation.

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:43.170
And I found myself narrating,

00:50:43.170 --> 00:50:44.550
as I'm going through all these boxes,

00:50:44.550 --> 00:50:45.900
as I would a correspondent

00:50:45.900 --> 00:50:47.640
if I was like writing this story,

00:50:47.640 --> 00:50:50.370
I was sort of narrating
in my head this stuff.

00:50:50.370 --> 00:50:52.110
And I just started recording it

00:50:52.110 --> 00:50:56.880
and ended up putting this
together with some interviews

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:00.030
with some people who
discussed loss and grief

00:51:00.030 --> 00:51:02.040
in ways that blew my mind.

00:51:02.040 --> 00:51:03.990
And one of them was Stephen Colbert,

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:07.440
whose father and two younger brothers

00:51:07.440 --> 00:51:09.480
were killed in an Eastern
Airlines plane crash

00:51:09.480 --> 00:51:11.700
when Stephen was 10-years-old.

00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:14.190
And Stephen talks about grief in a way

00:51:14.190 --> 00:51:16.293
that I've never heard before.

00:51:17.670 --> 00:51:20.310
And I'd done a brief interview
with him years ago about it.

00:51:20.310 --> 00:51:22.560
And so, he's in the podcast

00:51:22.560 --> 00:51:23.640
and it's all these people,

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:27.570
it's Lori Anderson, who's
a really cool musician,

00:51:27.570 --> 00:51:30.810
Elizabeth Alexander, who was here at Yale.

00:51:30.810 --> 00:51:34.470
So it's just different ways of
thinking about grief and loss

00:51:34.470 --> 00:51:36.540
and it's, yeah,

00:51:36.540 --> 00:51:38.853
I've been surprised at the response to it.

00:51:38.853 --> 00:51:40.473
It's been pretty extraordinary.

00:51:41.430 --> 00:51:43.260
- I think, probably,

00:51:43.260 --> 00:51:45.210
especially in this moment in time

00:51:45.210 --> 00:51:47.700
when we're coming outta Covid,

00:51:47.700 --> 00:51:49.470
I mean, obviously, as you've said,

00:51:49.470 --> 00:51:52.560
death is something that we all go through,

00:51:52.560 --> 00:51:55.530
having to survive it,
deal with the aftermath.

00:51:55.530 --> 00:51:56.730
But I think we're in a place,

00:51:56.730 --> 00:51:59.080
maybe in American society and the world

00:52:00.125 --> 00:52:01.530
that has been dealing with so much death

00:52:01.530 --> 00:52:03.900
and maybe your podcast is coming along

00:52:03.900 --> 00:52:05.130
at a time that we all need it

00:52:05.130 --> 00:52:08.176
to really find our way through it.

00:52:08.176 --> 00:52:10.376
- Yeah, I don't know about that, but.

00:52:10.376 --> 00:52:11.250
(audience laughing)
- I think so.

00:52:11.250 --> 00:52:12.420
- I wouldn't say, we all need it,

00:52:12.420 --> 00:52:14.170
I mean, it's just a little podcast,

00:52:15.633 --> 00:52:18.960
but no, what's nice is that,

00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:20.103
I mean, for me,

00:52:21.810 --> 00:52:23.830
it has made me feel less alone

00:52:25.351 --> 00:52:27.570
in this kind of bizarre world of grief

00:52:27.570 --> 00:52:30.090
to realize that I'm on this road

00:52:30.090 --> 00:52:33.993
that everybody will be on at some point.

00:52:34.830 --> 00:52:38.322
And generations of people
have been here before.

00:52:38.322 --> 00:52:39.690
You know, we all,

00:52:39.690 --> 00:52:43.470
I remember at Yale, and
it's a perfect example,

00:52:43.470 --> 00:52:44.730
anybody's who's an undergraduate here,

00:52:44.730 --> 00:52:46.350
you all live in rooms of dead people,

00:52:46.350 --> 00:52:49.500
you all live in the rooms
of generations of people

00:52:49.500 --> 00:52:51.330
who have been in those rooms before.

00:52:51.330 --> 00:52:54.120
And we all think, Robert Henline said,

00:52:54.120 --> 00:52:56.190
every generation thinks they invented sex

00:52:56.190 --> 00:52:57.930
and every generation is wrong.

00:52:57.930 --> 00:52:59.550
You know, we all think we're the first

00:52:59.550 --> 00:53:02.280
to like have these
experiences that we're having

00:53:02.280 --> 00:53:05.640
and to be facing the issues we're facing.

00:53:05.640 --> 00:53:09.567
And as one ages, you start
to realize like, I mean,

00:53:09.567 --> 00:53:13.830
and it's an obvious thing,
but there are, everybody,

00:53:13.830 --> 00:53:16.500
there's not anything you are
going through in your life

00:53:16.500 --> 00:53:18.180
that has not already been gone through

00:53:18.180 --> 00:53:20.400
by generations of people before you.

00:53:20.400 --> 00:53:22.200
And I take great comfort in that.

00:53:22.200 --> 00:53:25.680
Like, it literally helps
me when I'm in a situation

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:28.080
where I feel completely overwhelmed

00:53:28.080 --> 00:53:30.810
or like my career is about to be ruined

00:53:30.810 --> 00:53:34.530
or my life is about to be
destroyed or whatever it is,

00:53:34.530 --> 00:53:38.190
and realize like somebody has
been in this situation before

00:53:38.190 --> 00:53:39.420
and there's a way out of it

00:53:39.420 --> 00:53:41.790
and things will take their course

00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:44.460
and grief is the same way.

00:53:44.460 --> 00:53:47.790
You know, there's not any
permutation of my grief

00:53:47.790 --> 00:53:50.250
that is original or different,

00:53:50.250 --> 00:53:51.780
and yet it feels that way to me,

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:53.550
and I feel completely alone in it,

00:53:53.550 --> 00:53:56.040
but realizing that,

00:53:56.040 --> 00:53:59.250
realizing all these people
who have been here before

00:53:59.250 --> 00:54:00.330
is going through the same things.

00:54:00.330 --> 00:54:02.790
I follow on Instagram, the AIDS Memorial.

00:54:02.790 --> 00:54:06.160
And to me, it's the most
incredible thing on Instagram

00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:13.443
and it's just photographs
of people have died,

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:17.370
put up by their loved ones
with little stories about them.

00:54:17.370 --> 00:54:22.370
And I see myself in that
page, every couple of days.

00:54:22.770 --> 00:54:26.670
I'm like, that's me, or
that's my friend, Joe,

00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:27.600
or whatever it is.

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:31.110
And it's just these generations of people

00:54:31.110 --> 00:54:32.100
who are no longer here,

00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:33.690
but who went through the exact same stuff

00:54:33.690 --> 00:54:35.940
and I find great comfort in that.

00:54:35.940 --> 00:54:37.200
Maybe I'm just weird.

00:54:37.200 --> 00:54:38.730
- No, no, no.

00:54:38.730 --> 00:54:41.520
I will say that for people
who are not quite prepared

00:54:41.520 --> 00:54:44.340
to be part of that conversation,

00:54:44.340 --> 00:54:46.440
it is quite an emotional
experience, I will say.

00:54:46.440 --> 00:54:50.670
Like, I clicked on it just to
see what you were doing and-

00:54:50.670 --> 00:54:52.787
- No, no, it'll like stop
you dead in your tracks.

00:54:52.787 --> 00:54:53.946
- I will stop you dead in your tracks.

00:54:53.946 --> 00:54:54.779
- And like make you weak.

00:54:54.779 --> 00:54:55.612
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:54:55.612 --> 00:54:58.905
No, but I like, there's
nothing wrong with that.

00:54:58.905 --> 00:55:00.942
Like, for me-
- No, I think it's so needed.

00:55:00.942 --> 00:55:01.775
- Yeah.

00:55:01.775 --> 00:55:02.910
- 'Cause I think, we're so in this space

00:55:02.910 --> 00:55:04.560
of we just have to keep going.

00:55:04.560 --> 00:55:06.030
We don't have time.
- Yeah.

00:55:06.030 --> 00:55:08.760
- But I think you're telling
us, we should make time.

00:55:08.760 --> 00:55:10.740
It's very important.
- Yeah, I mean.

00:55:10.740 --> 00:55:12.510
- Grief is not something to-

00:55:12.510 --> 00:55:15.780
- Yeah, look, I wasn't
able to talk about loss

00:55:15.780 --> 00:55:17.010
for my entire life.

00:55:17.010 --> 00:55:20.100
And my brother died
right before senior year,

00:55:20.100 --> 00:55:24.740
and I mean, I was
stunned and completely...

00:55:25.710 --> 00:55:27.960
Senior year, I was like, I
don't know what I'm doing.

00:55:27.960 --> 00:55:30.273
I didn't apply for any jobs.

00:55:31.260 --> 00:55:32.100
Like, I graduated.

00:55:32.100 --> 00:55:34.350
I had no idea what was gonna happen,

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:35.463
what I was gonna do.

00:55:38.940 --> 00:55:40.233
So yeah, I think,

00:55:41.340 --> 00:55:42.690
yeah, I don't know.

00:55:42.690 --> 00:55:45.123
I certainly would've benefited from,

00:55:46.650 --> 00:55:48.510
I wish I had been able to talk about it.

00:55:48.510 --> 00:55:49.860
It's not that I didn't know I should,

00:55:49.860 --> 00:55:52.133
I certainly knew I should,
and, but yeah, I didn't.

00:55:55.500 --> 00:55:58.350
- So maybe this is not
the best place to end,

00:55:58.350 --> 00:55:59.548
but I do realize that-

00:55:59.548 --> 00:56:00.735
(Anderson and Mary laughing)

00:56:00.735 --> 00:56:01.650
- We're really bringing it down here.

00:56:01.650 --> 00:56:04.383
- No, I'm hoping the students
will bring us back up.

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:07.620
We're in the portion, I'm
just mindful of the show.

00:56:07.620 --> 00:56:08.453
- Sure, yeah.

00:56:08.453 --> 00:56:11.550
- We do need to get to our Q&amp;A.

00:56:11.550 --> 00:56:14.010
And we did have five students

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:16.743
who had submitted questions that we are,

00:56:18.270 --> 00:56:19.980
I think we have time for.

00:56:19.980 --> 00:56:23.490
So if the five students
could sort of stand up

00:56:23.490 --> 00:56:25.730
and raise their hands so that our...

00:56:27.570 --> 00:56:30.087
Excellent, you're all very
close together, kind of.

00:56:30.087 --> 00:56:32.206
Megha is all the way out there.

00:56:32.206 --> 00:56:33.210
- This is really like the honor system.

00:56:33.210 --> 00:56:34.890
I thought like 20 other
people would be like,

00:56:34.890 --> 00:56:36.105
oh yeah, I'm one of the. (laughing)

00:56:36.105 --> 00:56:38.130
(audience laughing)

00:56:38.130 --> 00:56:39.900
- This is what happens
when you're in a college

00:56:39.900 --> 00:56:42.095
where the head of college
knows who everybody is.

00:56:42.095 --> 00:56:42.928
(Anderson, audience laughing)

00:56:42.928 --> 00:56:44.323
- Nobody's trying to fake it out.

00:56:44.323 --> 00:56:45.900
- Smart.

00:56:45.900 --> 00:56:48.150
- But yes, we can go in this direction.

00:56:48.150 --> 00:56:49.320
Megha, you wanna be first?

00:56:49.320 --> 00:56:50.223
Go for it.

00:56:54.570 --> 00:56:56.010
- [Megha] Hi, thank you so much

00:56:56.010 --> 00:56:57.150
for being here with us today.

00:56:57.150 --> 00:56:58.110
- Hey!

00:56:58.110 --> 00:56:59.370
- [Megha] This feels like really unreal

00:56:59.370 --> 00:57:00.840
to just be able to talk to you.

00:57:00.840 --> 00:57:04.590
- Me too.
(Mary, audience laughing)

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:05.700
- My name's Megha Joshi.

00:57:05.700 --> 00:57:08.070
I'm a senior in Timothy Dwight College.

00:57:08.070 --> 00:57:09.180
And I was wondering,

00:57:09.180 --> 00:57:12.780
how do you feel that large
news networks such as CNN

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:15.300
affect the polarization of America?

00:57:15.300 --> 00:57:18.060
And what do you do in your
profession specifically

00:57:18.060 --> 00:57:19.890
to try and minimize it?

00:57:19.890 --> 00:57:20.990
- Yeah, look, I think,

00:57:22.020 --> 00:57:24.120
we live in an incredibly
polarized society.

00:57:24.120 --> 00:57:28.050
I think it's been polarized
for a very long time,

00:57:28.050 --> 00:57:30.630
certainly, any reading
of history shows that.

00:57:30.630 --> 00:57:34.320
And our media has also,

00:57:34.320 --> 00:57:38.100
if you look at, Alexander
Hamilton started the New York Post

00:57:38.100 --> 00:57:40.623
in order to get his point across.

00:57:44.166 --> 00:57:45.393
I think it's difficult,

00:57:46.320 --> 00:57:47.610
I think it's something,

00:57:47.610 --> 00:57:49.860
it's certainly something I
think about all the time.

00:57:49.860 --> 00:57:51.960
It's something I worry about all the time,

00:57:52.860 --> 00:57:56.610
and I know everybody in
my orbit who I work with,

00:57:56.610 --> 00:57:58.533
it's a great concern as well.

00:58:00.360 --> 00:58:03.960
You know, I try not to,

00:58:03.960 --> 00:58:08.130
I'm not interested in
being a liberal newscast

00:58:08.130 --> 00:58:09.570
or a conservative newscast.

00:58:09.570 --> 00:58:13.050
I don't watch partisan newscast like that.

00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:17.070
I'm not interested in what
some overpaid anchor thinks

00:58:17.070 --> 00:58:20.310
about what candidate they support

00:58:20.310 --> 00:58:22.323
or what policy they like.

00:58:23.340 --> 00:58:25.230
Like, I'm not interested in that.

00:58:25.230 --> 00:58:27.540
I think our viewers, you know,

00:58:27.540 --> 00:58:29.280
should make up their
own minds about stuff.

00:58:29.280 --> 00:58:30.720
I believe in facts,

00:58:30.720 --> 00:58:33.180
and I believe in presenting
people with facts

00:58:33.180 --> 00:58:36.237
and talking to people from
different walks of life

00:58:36.237 --> 00:58:38.070
and different political positions

00:58:38.070 --> 00:58:40.503
and letting viewers make up their mind.

00:58:42.677 --> 00:58:46.923
I do think in an age
where, when people lie,

00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:51.780
I think it's the job of
journalists to point that out,

00:58:51.780 --> 00:58:52.980
that they are lying.

00:58:52.980 --> 00:58:54.963
That can also, at times,

00:58:58.350 --> 00:58:59.970
many people think they are doing that,

00:58:59.970 --> 00:59:02.070
and that can be very
partisan in some ways,

00:59:02.070 --> 00:59:03.690
if you believe,

00:59:03.690 --> 00:59:04.740
if you are seeing things

00:59:04.740 --> 00:59:06.540
through a certain ideological prism,

00:59:06.540 --> 00:59:08.700
and you are confronting
people based on your prism,

00:59:08.700 --> 00:59:11.010
and you think you're doing
it because it's true,

00:59:11.010 --> 00:59:12.360
and what they're saying is false,

00:59:12.360 --> 00:59:14.070
then that can be partisan.

00:59:14.070 --> 00:59:15.330
So I think these are all things

00:59:15.330 --> 00:59:17.520
you have to be constantly conscious of

00:59:17.520 --> 00:59:21.600
and constantly concerned about bias,

00:59:21.600 --> 00:59:23.730
and bias in many different forms,

00:59:23.730 --> 00:59:27.870
not just political bias, but economic.

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:29.580
The lens through which we see,

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:31.740
everybody sees things through a lens,

00:59:31.740 --> 00:59:35.160
and that lens is influenced by geography

00:59:35.160 --> 00:59:36.240
of where you're from,

00:59:36.240 --> 00:59:37.800
the background you have,

00:59:37.800 --> 00:59:40.230
the political beliefs
you have, whatever it is.

00:59:40.230 --> 00:59:42.570
There are many kinds of biases.

00:59:42.570 --> 00:59:45.270
And I think it's important
to be aware of all of them.

00:59:49.020 --> 00:59:51.480
I do think we are living in an age

00:59:51.480 --> 00:59:54.180
where there's so much information

00:59:54.180 --> 00:59:56.340
and you know, when I was growing up,

00:59:56.340 --> 00:59:58.800
it was three broadcast news channels,

00:59:58.800 --> 01:00:03.120
and it was ABC, CBS, and NBC,

01:00:03.120 --> 01:00:04.230
and Walter Cronkite,

01:00:04.230 --> 01:00:05.790
and I don't know who else
was on the other ones.

01:00:05.790 --> 01:00:07.800
Everyone watched Walter
Cronkite or Roger Mudd,

01:00:07.800 --> 01:00:09.450
I guess, for a while, was on NBC.

01:00:11.553 --> 01:00:13.620
And you know, people now,

01:00:13.620 --> 01:00:15.390
a lot of people from
my generation and older

01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:16.470
look back at that and think,

01:00:16.470 --> 01:00:18.300
oh, that was the golden age of news.

01:00:18.300 --> 01:00:19.710
Like, we trusted those people.

01:00:19.710 --> 01:00:22.770
Those people were good, we trusted them.

01:00:22.770 --> 01:00:24.630
But if you actually look back,

01:00:24.630 --> 01:00:27.330
like the original newscasts
were 18 minutes long.

01:00:27.330 --> 01:00:30.120
And so, that's what people,

01:00:30.120 --> 01:00:31.560
people thought they were getting,

01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:33.330
in 18 minutes, they
thought they were getting

01:00:33.330 --> 01:00:34.880
everything they needed to know.

01:00:35.749 --> 01:00:36.582
That's the way it was,

01:00:36.582 --> 01:00:37.950
was Walter Cronkite's tagline.

01:00:38.790 --> 01:00:39.990
And I have great respect for him.

01:00:39.990 --> 01:00:41.040
I think he was an amazing person.

01:00:41.040 --> 01:00:43.620
But the newsrooms were all white,

01:00:43.620 --> 01:00:45.690
they were all male,

01:00:45.690 --> 01:00:48.123
all generally New York, Washington.

01:00:51.366 --> 01:00:54.300
There weren't people of color, gay people.

01:00:54.300 --> 01:00:56.933
I mean, it was a very

01:00:58.920 --> 01:01:01.170
small lens that you were
seeing things through.

01:01:01.170 --> 01:01:03.650
And I think it's easy to look for people,

01:01:03.650 --> 01:01:05.310
of a certain generation,
to look back on that

01:01:05.310 --> 01:01:07.170
and think, oh, that was a time

01:01:07.170 --> 01:01:10.443
where you could really
trust what you were seeing.

01:01:11.310 --> 01:01:14.040
I actually think we're living
in this extraordinary time

01:01:14.040 --> 01:01:17.147
of information where we
all have, in this room,

01:01:17.147 --> 01:01:18.960
and on our phones,

01:01:18.960 --> 01:01:22.560
access to more information than anybody

01:01:22.560 --> 01:01:26.193
in the history of the world
has ever had access to.

01:01:27.510 --> 01:01:28.890
And I think that's an amazing thing.

01:01:28.890 --> 01:01:32.040
I think it's also overwhelming.

01:01:32.040 --> 01:01:34.170
I think it contributes to this feeling

01:01:34.170 --> 01:01:35.970
that the world is just
getting worse and worse

01:01:35.970 --> 01:01:38.640
and like things are so out of control.

01:01:38.640 --> 01:01:40.170
I actually think that
the opposite is true.

01:01:40.170 --> 01:01:42.627
I think the world is better
than it's ever been before.

01:01:42.627 --> 01:01:44.850
And I think if you look at the data,

01:01:44.850 --> 01:01:46.110
just about every metric,

01:01:46.110 --> 01:01:48.090
your life is way better than anybody

01:01:48.090 --> 01:01:49.320
who's ever lived on the planet.

01:01:49.320 --> 01:01:52.350
And your future, I mean, obviously,

01:01:52.350 --> 01:01:56.130
there's tremendous climate change,

01:01:56.130 --> 01:01:58.290
all sorts of things to be concerned about.

01:01:58.290 --> 01:02:03.150
But literacy rates, life expectancy,

01:02:03.150 --> 01:02:06.210
I mean, by just about every metric,

01:02:06.210 --> 01:02:07.620
life is better than it's ever been

01:02:07.620 --> 01:02:08.580
and it's only getting better.

01:02:08.580 --> 01:02:11.640
So it's a long way of answering.

01:02:11.640 --> 01:02:13.293
I think it's a real issue.

01:02:14.490 --> 01:02:16.710
I try not to contribute to polarization.

01:02:16.710 --> 01:02:21.240
I try not to demonize
people, as Jimmy was saying.

01:02:21.240 --> 01:02:25.187
I try not to fall into this
sort of left right thing.

01:02:25.187 --> 01:02:28.440
I try to talk to people
from different backgrounds

01:02:28.440 --> 01:02:29.850
and understand where they're coming from

01:02:29.850 --> 01:02:31.110
and present that to viewers.

01:02:31.110 --> 01:02:32.360
That's what I believe in.

01:02:33.840 --> 01:02:35.100
- Thank you.

01:02:35.100 --> 01:02:36.870
Okay, who?

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:37.980
Come back, pop back up.

01:02:37.980 --> 01:02:38.813
Who was...

01:02:40.530 --> 01:02:42.243
I guess, Caitlin, you're next.

01:02:43.770 --> 01:02:44.670
- Hello.
- Hi.

01:02:44.670 --> 01:02:45.570
- [Caitlin] My name is Caitlin.

01:02:45.570 --> 01:02:48.060
I'm a sophomore in TD.
- Cool.

01:02:48.060 --> 01:02:49.140
- And I was curious,

01:02:49.140 --> 01:02:51.060
so you've covered a lifetime of stories,

01:02:51.060 --> 01:02:53.100
but is there one story you feel

01:02:53.100 --> 01:02:56.340
that you covered that
didn't receive the attention

01:02:56.340 --> 01:02:59.490
or the emotional reaction that it merited?

01:02:59.490 --> 01:03:03.300
And if so, how did this
experience change your perspective

01:03:03.300 --> 01:03:05.190
on our media consumption?

01:03:05.190 --> 01:03:06.023
- Hmm.

01:03:08.640 --> 01:03:10.430
Yeah, look, I...

01:03:12.060 --> 01:03:14.070
Yeah, a lot of the
stuff I'm interested in,

01:03:14.070 --> 01:03:17.250
people aren't interested in so I get that.

01:03:17.250 --> 01:03:18.750
And that's okay.

01:03:18.750 --> 01:03:20.940
You know, people have a lot
going on in their lives,

01:03:20.940 --> 01:03:24.120
and so, I will give
you a specific example,

01:03:24.120 --> 01:03:26.130
but I don't wanna sound
like I'm pontificating like,

01:03:26.130 --> 01:03:28.800
oh, if only people will
listen to me talk about this,

01:03:28.800 --> 01:03:30.660
everything will be fine.

01:03:30.660 --> 01:03:32.580
People are busy, they have lives going on.

01:03:32.580 --> 01:03:33.480
I totally get that.

01:03:33.480 --> 01:03:37.350
And everybody has a right to think about

01:03:37.350 --> 01:03:40.440
what they feel is important
and find that information out.

01:03:40.440 --> 01:03:42.810
And I'm glad we live in an
age where we can find out

01:03:42.810 --> 01:03:45.510
whatever information you wanna
find out, you can find out.

01:03:45.510 --> 01:03:47.130
You know, the things that interest me,

01:03:47.130 --> 01:03:48.270
I'm drawn to places

01:03:48.270 --> 01:03:49.710
that a lot of people
don't pay attention to.

01:03:49.710 --> 01:03:52.620
I'm drawn to Democratic Republic of Congo.

01:03:52.620 --> 01:03:54.723
I will come back to.

01:03:56.112 --> 01:03:58.350
I am fascinated by that country.

01:03:58.350 --> 01:03:59.433
I'm fascinated.

01:04:03.300 --> 01:04:05.790
There's a tremendous, yeah.

01:04:05.790 --> 01:04:08.310
I mean, violence in Eastern
Democratic Republic of Congo

01:04:08.310 --> 01:04:10.080
is something I'm very interested in.

01:04:10.080 --> 01:04:12.810
And some of the most extraordinary people

01:04:12.810 --> 01:04:14.970
I've ever talked have been,

01:04:14.970 --> 01:04:17.213
I talked to a woman
named Angela who was...

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:25.380
She was brutally assaulted in her village

01:04:25.380 --> 01:04:28.860
by government forces who
came into the village.

01:04:28.860 --> 01:04:32.760
She was sexually assaulted
by a number of people.

01:04:32.760 --> 01:04:34.830
They tried to get her
brother involved in it.

01:04:34.830 --> 01:04:37.770
He resisted, so they slit
his throat in front of her.

01:04:37.770 --> 01:04:40.383
They threw one of her children on a fire.

01:04:41.430 --> 01:04:42.870
Then after they left,

01:04:42.870 --> 01:04:45.420
her husband came back and
kicked her out of the village

01:04:45.420 --> 01:04:47.460
because he was afraid
she was HIV positive.

01:04:47.460 --> 01:04:49.020
And so, when I met her,

01:04:49.020 --> 01:04:52.230
she was living in another
village with her children.

01:04:52.230 --> 01:04:55.350
Her child who was thrown
on the fire survived.

01:04:55.350 --> 01:04:58.860
And she was waking up every
day and trying to find work,

01:04:58.860 --> 01:05:03.540
and she was living and surviving.

01:05:03.540 --> 01:05:08.540
And so, that's somebody like
I think about all the time.

01:05:12.660 --> 01:05:17.660
Yeah, I wish I could have
done more for that person

01:05:17.940 --> 01:05:19.290
in her life to change her life,

01:05:19.290 --> 01:05:20.820
and also to tell her story

01:05:20.820 --> 01:05:23.460
in a way that more people
would see and watch.

01:05:23.460 --> 01:05:27.330
But in all honesty, she
didn't speak English.

01:05:27.330 --> 01:05:29.070
And that's difficult for some viewers.

01:05:29.070 --> 01:05:31.167
And it's a tough story

01:05:31.167 --> 01:05:32.937
and it's easy to turn the channel on,

01:05:32.937 --> 01:05:34.830
and I totally understand
that, and I get that.

01:05:34.830 --> 01:05:37.440
But for me, I think that's when I,

01:05:37.440 --> 01:05:39.630
it's weird to even bring those things up,

01:05:39.630 --> 01:05:42.780
but when you're trying to
tell somebody's stories,

01:05:42.780 --> 01:05:44.310
these are all things
you have to think about

01:05:44.310 --> 01:05:46.260
in terms of how you present the story

01:05:46.260 --> 01:05:49.020
and what is the best way
to get that person's story

01:05:49.020 --> 01:05:50.940
to an audience.

01:05:50.940 --> 01:05:52.770
And hopefully people see it

01:05:52.770 --> 01:05:55.590
and want to help the
organization who is helping her,

01:05:55.590 --> 01:05:56.700
which is how I found her.

01:05:56.700 --> 01:05:57.633
And so,

01:05:59.280 --> 01:06:01.070
yeah, I think that's...

01:06:02.190 --> 01:06:03.120
There's so many stories

01:06:03.120 --> 01:06:05.160
we don't pay attention to that I wish,

01:06:05.160 --> 01:06:06.960
there's a lot of
organizations that I work with

01:06:06.960 --> 01:06:08.130
after doing stories on them

01:06:08.130 --> 01:06:09.810
because I think they're doing great work

01:06:09.810 --> 01:06:11.460
and I want to continue that work.

01:06:12.300 --> 01:06:15.273
So, yeah, I dunno if that
answers your question, but.

01:06:20.820 --> 01:06:22.796
- Hi, Anderson.
- Hey.

01:06:22.796 --> 01:06:24.660
- [Arlene] (nervously
laughs) Thank you so much

01:06:24.660 --> 01:06:26.070
for being here with us today.

01:06:26.070 --> 01:06:28.440
My name's Arlene, and
I'm a sophomore in TD.

01:06:28.440 --> 01:06:31.260
And my question is, as
a television journalist,

01:06:31.260 --> 01:06:34.290
what has been the biggest
challenge you have faced on CNN

01:06:34.290 --> 01:06:35.950
and how did you navigate it

01:06:37.489 --> 01:06:38.520
in order to continue

01:06:38.520 --> 01:06:41.100
reporting to all the people watching you?

01:06:41.100 --> 01:06:42.570
- Hmm.

01:06:42.570 --> 01:06:44.270
What's been the biggest challenge?

01:06:47.520 --> 01:06:50.003
Hmm, I mean, there's a lot.

01:06:54.630 --> 01:06:59.630
There's personal, like handling
stress, things like that.

01:06:59.760 --> 01:07:01.650
But I guess more,

01:07:01.650 --> 01:07:04.953
look, I think certainly in
the last administration,

01:07:05.790 --> 01:07:09.630
it was a challenging
environment and continues to be,

01:07:09.630 --> 01:07:11.100
but certainly last administration

01:07:11.100 --> 01:07:12.550
was a challenging environment

01:07:14.130 --> 01:07:16.703
to be targeted by the
president of the United States,

01:07:16.703 --> 01:07:18.630
to have the company I worked for

01:07:18.630 --> 01:07:21.690
targeted by the president
of the United States.

01:07:21.690 --> 01:07:26.640
And two, it's been an
interesting thing to see

01:07:26.640 --> 01:07:29.670
the kind of techniques now being used

01:07:29.670 --> 01:07:33.240
by people in public life,

01:07:33.240 --> 01:07:34.350
in political life,

01:07:34.350 --> 01:07:35.730
to not answer questions

01:07:35.730 --> 01:07:39.420
or to just only speak to outlets

01:07:39.420 --> 01:07:42.783
that they feel represent
their ideological slant.

01:07:44.070 --> 01:07:46.803
And I think as reporters, it's been,

01:07:48.810 --> 01:07:51.730
I've worked overseas in regimes

01:07:53.886 --> 01:07:55.450
where truth is

01:07:58.530 --> 01:08:00.650
not something that you get...

01:08:02.550 --> 01:08:04.200
How can I say this?

01:08:04.200 --> 01:08:08.040
You know, which are not truthful.

01:08:08.040 --> 01:08:10.950
And it's difficult to figure out

01:08:10.950 --> 01:08:12.990
how to work in those environments,

01:08:12.990 --> 01:08:16.290
how to respond in a way that doesn't,

01:08:16.290 --> 01:08:17.460
it's easier to overreact

01:08:17.460 --> 01:08:18.873
and it's easier to kind of,

01:08:21.720 --> 01:08:24.780
yeah, I'll just say it
was just a difficult time.

01:08:24.780 --> 01:08:27.720
To me, personally, that was
the hardest thing, to not,

01:08:27.720 --> 01:08:31.590
you know, 'cause I don't want
to express personal opinions.

01:08:31.590 --> 01:08:34.920
I'm not interested in being somebody

01:08:34.920 --> 01:08:36.540
who wears their opinion on their sleeve

01:08:36.540 --> 01:08:39.030
and tries to force it on viewers.

01:08:39.030 --> 01:08:40.950
I do believe in listening to other people

01:08:40.950 --> 01:08:44.850
and being prepared for interviews

01:08:44.850 --> 01:08:47.010
so that you can factually
push back on somebody.

01:08:47.010 --> 01:08:49.740
But I don't argue with
people over their politics.

01:08:49.740 --> 01:08:52.113
Good people can see
things in different ways.

01:08:52.980 --> 01:08:55.560
And there can be some truth on both sides,

01:08:55.560 --> 01:08:59.400
but there are facts and I try to stick to,

01:08:59.400 --> 01:09:00.970
if I'm in a contentious

01:09:02.910 --> 01:09:04.260
interview with somebody,

01:09:04.260 --> 01:09:05.490
I try to stick to facts

01:09:05.490 --> 01:09:07.050
and what is factually correct

01:09:07.050 --> 01:09:10.470
and what they've said that
is not factually correct.

01:09:10.470 --> 01:09:13.740
And to me, that's the ground I like

01:09:13.740 --> 01:09:16.263
to try to confront somebody on.

01:09:23.160 --> 01:09:24.090
- [Carlos] Hello, Mr. Cooper.

01:09:24.090 --> 01:09:26.250
I'm Carlos, you kind of just
answered a bit of my question,

01:09:26.250 --> 01:09:28.710
but I just wanted to ask like,

01:09:28.710 --> 01:09:31.200
how do you separate your
own feelings and opinions

01:09:31.200 --> 01:09:33.180
from all the things that you cover?

01:09:33.180 --> 01:09:34.140
And I know you just said

01:09:34.140 --> 01:09:35.460
you don't like to share your opinion,

01:09:35.460 --> 01:09:37.410
but do you think there's like value

01:09:37.410 --> 01:09:38.700
in putting your opinion out there

01:09:38.700 --> 01:09:41.040
and being honest with the
public of what you think?

01:09:41.040 --> 01:09:41.873
- Right.

01:09:43.290 --> 01:09:44.123
So...

01:09:46.260 --> 01:09:48.360
I don't, look, we live in an age

01:09:48.360 --> 01:09:49.800
where everybody is
expressing their opinion.

01:09:49.800 --> 01:09:51.060
And I think that's great.

01:09:51.060 --> 01:09:54.303
I think it's great but I do
think that there's a role,

01:09:56.010 --> 01:09:56.843
you know?

01:09:57.717 --> 01:09:59.463
I don't think I'm best at,

01:10:00.510 --> 01:10:02.100
like, I don't, yeah.

01:10:02.100 --> 01:10:05.730
I don't believe in getting up every night

01:10:05.730 --> 01:10:08.790
and expressing my opinion on issues

01:10:08.790 --> 01:10:11.490
which affect people's lives
that I may not understand

01:10:11.490 --> 01:10:16.200
how somebody with lived
experience sees something

01:10:16.200 --> 01:10:17.610
or has experienced something.

01:10:17.610 --> 01:10:22.590
And so, I'm not egotistical
enough to believe that,

01:10:22.590 --> 01:10:23.423
I'm egotistical but,

01:10:23.423 --> 01:10:25.320
I'm egotistical enough to be on TV

01:10:25.320 --> 01:10:27.780
but not enough to believe
that I know everything

01:10:27.780 --> 01:10:28.800
or that I'm right.

01:10:28.800 --> 01:10:32.520
And so, I very much
view myself as learning,

01:10:32.520 --> 01:10:34.050
just as our viewers are learning.

01:10:34.050 --> 01:10:35.940
And I am generally asking questions

01:10:35.940 --> 01:10:39.900
that I'm actually interested
in hearing the answers to.

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:43.200
I'm not interviewing people
who I know reflect a certain,

01:10:43.200 --> 01:10:44.610
you know, echo chamber,

01:10:44.610 --> 01:10:47.220
that they're gonna give
me a political answer

01:10:47.220 --> 01:10:48.480
that is gonna please me

01:10:48.480 --> 01:10:51.570
so that my viewers will
only be exposed to some

01:10:51.570 --> 01:10:53.733
pleasing point of view.

01:10:58.144 --> 01:11:00.900
I believe in being honest
in all things I do,

01:11:00.900 --> 01:11:02.010
but it doesn't necessarily mean

01:11:02.010 --> 01:11:06.213
that I have to announce ahead of time,

01:11:07.260 --> 01:11:09.360
everything I believe in.

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:10.473
I think you can,

01:11:12.450 --> 01:11:13.980
if you're focused on facts,

01:11:13.980 --> 01:11:16.470
if you're focused on a
world of what is true

01:11:16.470 --> 01:11:17.430
and what is not true,

01:11:17.430 --> 01:11:21.183
as opposed to shades of gray and opinion,

01:11:22.350 --> 01:11:25.320
for me, that's where I, frankly,

01:11:25.320 --> 01:11:28.140
I think there's not
enough people doing that.

01:11:28.140 --> 01:11:32.043
And so, that is where
I choose to focus on.

01:11:35.850 --> 01:11:38.300
Yeah, I dunno if that
answers your question, but.

01:11:41.100 --> 01:11:42.660
- Hi Anderson, thank you.
- Hey.

01:11:42.660 --> 01:11:45.060
- [Quinn] I'm Quinn, I'm a freshman in TD.

01:11:45.060 --> 01:11:46.740
In the past five years,

01:11:46.740 --> 01:11:48.810
there's been a pretty sharp increase

01:11:48.810 --> 01:11:51.330
in the amount of
misinformation and false news

01:11:51.330 --> 01:11:54.780
that's circulated on the
internet and other sources.

01:11:54.780 --> 01:11:58.920
And this has led to a
pretty large social shift

01:11:58.920 --> 01:12:01.230
in terms of how American,

01:12:01.230 --> 01:12:03.730
the American public
views news organizations

01:12:04.800 --> 01:12:07.200
with a large portion of the population

01:12:07.200 --> 01:12:09.660
solely viewing those outlets

01:12:09.660 --> 01:12:12.030
that align with their opinions

01:12:12.030 --> 01:12:15.870
and decrying others as fake news.

01:12:15.870 --> 01:12:19.710
And with this in mind,

01:12:19.710 --> 01:12:22.650
how can news organizations and journalists

01:12:22.650 --> 01:12:25.410
individually work to combat this problem

01:12:25.410 --> 01:12:27.990
and reinstall trust in the news?

01:12:27.990 --> 01:12:30.423
- Hmm, look, I think,

01:12:34.530 --> 01:12:35.670
I'll speak for myself.

01:12:35.670 --> 01:12:40.110
I think it's important
to do the best you can

01:12:40.110 --> 01:12:41.400
and to not be a jerk

01:12:41.400 --> 01:12:45.030
and to not get things wrong

01:12:45.030 --> 01:12:47.860
and to not give into your biases

01:12:49.776 --> 01:12:50.760
and to think about all these things

01:12:50.760 --> 01:12:52.500
and to spend a lot of
time worrying about it

01:12:52.500 --> 01:12:53.400
and thinking about it.

01:12:53.400 --> 01:12:58.400
And I know I do and I know
the people around me do.

01:12:58.890 --> 01:12:59.730
I can't speak,

01:12:59.730 --> 01:13:01.950
I don't know about some
of these other folks

01:13:01.950 --> 01:13:04.113
in other channels and stuff.

01:13:05.520 --> 01:13:08.310
You know, it's fascinating
to see the Dominion lawsuit

01:13:08.310 --> 01:13:09.143
that's going forward.

01:13:09.143 --> 01:13:10.470
I don't know how closely
you've been following this,

01:13:10.470 --> 01:13:12.420
but to see sort of the text exchanges

01:13:12.420 --> 01:13:15.060
between other anchors and other network,

01:13:15.060 --> 01:13:17.760
concerned about the stock
price of the parent company,

01:13:19.080 --> 01:13:21.390
I don't know what my parent company,

01:13:21.390 --> 01:13:23.485
I'm not even sure who my
parent company is these days.

01:13:23.485 --> 01:13:24.318
(Mary, audience laughing)

01:13:24.318 --> 01:13:26.880
But like, I don't know
what the stock price is.

01:13:26.880 --> 01:13:30.360
It would never even occur to me to like,

01:13:30.360 --> 01:13:31.713
why would I care?

01:13:32.760 --> 01:13:36.450
And I'm sure my corporate
overlords, whoever they are,

01:13:36.450 --> 01:13:39.660
would probably not like that,
but that's just not my job.

01:13:39.660 --> 01:13:42.630
And so that was sort of,

01:13:42.630 --> 01:13:43.979
I'm not gonna say it surprised me

01:13:43.979 --> 01:13:45.270
'cause I know the people involved

01:13:45.270 --> 01:13:47.703
and that doesn't surprise me.

01:13:50.484 --> 01:13:53.070
But I think it's important

01:13:53.070 --> 01:13:55.200
that you have personal standards

01:13:55.200 --> 01:13:58.050
and personal things that you personally,

01:13:58.050 --> 01:14:00.624
what is right and what is
wrong and what your job is.

01:14:00.624 --> 01:14:01.680
You know, I know what my job is.

01:14:01.680 --> 01:14:04.320
My job is not to pontificate every night

01:14:04.320 --> 01:14:08.280
about what I think and
that's not why I'm there.

01:14:08.280 --> 01:14:10.680
I'm there 'cause I've spent a lifetime

01:14:10.680 --> 01:14:12.240
of telling other people's stories

01:14:12.240 --> 01:14:13.950
and going to places and seeing things

01:14:13.950 --> 01:14:16.230
and wanting to bring
viewers to those places

01:14:16.230 --> 01:14:18.750
so that they can walk in
somebody else's shoes.

01:14:18.750 --> 01:14:21.720
And so, that's the only thing
I add to this conversation

01:14:21.720 --> 01:14:23.139
and to add to the table.

01:14:23.139 --> 01:14:26.520
There's a lot of people
who do that other stuff

01:14:26.520 --> 01:14:27.510
really, really well.

01:14:27.510 --> 01:14:30.810
If you want like really smart
liberal people doing that

01:14:30.810 --> 01:14:32.940
and if you want really smart
conservative people doing that,

01:14:32.940 --> 01:14:35.010
they're out there and they have networks

01:14:35.010 --> 01:14:36.360
that are doing really well.

01:14:37.740 --> 01:14:39.120
It's just not what I,

01:14:39.120 --> 01:14:40.590
I'm just not that way personally

01:14:40.590 --> 01:14:41.970
and it just doesn't interest me.

01:14:41.970 --> 01:14:45.720
So my interest is,

01:14:45.720 --> 01:14:50.130
yeah, in just trying to
understand what is going on

01:14:50.130 --> 01:14:52.680
and trying to do it in a way that's human

01:14:52.680 --> 01:14:57.680
and not left or right, but
sort of center, I guess.

01:15:04.725 --> 01:15:07.020
It doesn't necessarily fit
with the times sometimes,

01:15:07.020 --> 01:15:12.020
but to me, that's what
I can do to try to help,

01:15:12.120 --> 01:15:14.100
to try to help on the polarization,

01:15:14.100 --> 01:15:18.303
to try to help on bias and misinformation.

01:15:20.610 --> 01:15:23.130
It's important that like
facts actually matter

01:15:23.130 --> 01:15:24.480
and there are things which are true

01:15:24.480 --> 01:15:25.500
and there are things which are not true.

01:15:25.500 --> 01:15:28.080
And we can actually go and
see the things which are true

01:15:28.080 --> 01:15:30.090
and we can tell you about
the things that are true

01:15:30.090 --> 01:15:32.880
and we can show you the
things that are not true.

01:15:32.880 --> 01:15:35.430
You know, I've interviewed people

01:15:35.430 --> 01:15:38.220
who believe I drink the blood of children

01:15:38.220 --> 01:15:40.468
and I am secretly a lizard.

01:15:40.468 --> 01:15:41.370
(Mary, audience laughing)

01:15:41.370 --> 01:15:44.010
And the former president
of the United States

01:15:44.010 --> 01:15:46.740
is catering to those people like that.

01:15:46.740 --> 01:15:48.030
They're all still out there

01:15:48.030 --> 01:15:49.830
and I mean, they literally believe

01:15:49.830 --> 01:15:52.140
I drink the blood of
children and, you know,

01:15:56.908 --> 01:15:58.006
I don't know what to
do with that, you know?

01:15:58.006 --> 01:16:00.104
(audience laughing)

01:16:00.104 --> 01:16:01.591
So, yeah.

01:16:01.591 --> 01:16:03.090
You know, we live in a time,

01:16:03.090 --> 01:16:03.990
it's interesting to me

01:16:03.990 --> 01:16:06.090
that the more information
we have access to,

01:16:06.090 --> 01:16:07.530
the more this has bubbled up

01:16:07.530 --> 01:16:10.260
and the more this
disinformation has spread.

01:16:10.260 --> 01:16:11.707
People can find out what is true.

01:16:11.707 --> 01:16:16.110
You know, people believe that
Joe Biden is a sock puppet.

01:16:16.110 --> 01:16:18.363
It's the same people who
believe I'm a lizard person.

01:16:18.363 --> 01:16:21.933
(Mary laughs)
So it's, yeah.

01:16:24.592 --> 01:16:25.980
I feel like I can do what I can do

01:16:25.980 --> 01:16:29.160
and I just don't, it's easy to get sucked

01:16:29.160 --> 01:16:30.690
into all that other stuff

01:16:30.690 --> 01:16:32.520
because there's a market incentive clearly

01:16:32.520 --> 01:16:34.800
for these other networks to do that.

01:16:34.800 --> 01:16:35.760
But it is just not something

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:37.660
that I personally am interested in so,

01:16:38.663 --> 01:16:39.496
or believe in.

01:16:39.496 --> 01:16:40.329
- So we're not gonna leave it there.

01:16:40.329 --> 01:16:42.090
- Okay.
- That would not be okay.

01:16:42.090 --> 01:16:43.500
- Okay.

01:16:43.500 --> 01:16:45.420
- So why don't we end by asking

01:16:45.420 --> 01:16:46.950
what are your future projects?

01:16:46.950 --> 01:16:49.887
So you have the Astor book
that's gonna finish out

01:16:49.887 --> 01:16:50.850
and what else?

01:16:50.850 --> 01:16:52.710
- Are you saying, I need another project?

01:16:52.710 --> 01:16:53.850
Is this-
(Mary, audience laughing)

01:16:53.850 --> 01:16:56.520
You're adding to my stress here.

01:16:56.520 --> 01:16:57.353
- Okay, let me help.

01:16:57.353 --> 01:17:00.007
- So no, so look, so I
work, yeah, I do CNN.

01:17:01.463 --> 01:17:03.330
I do 10 stories a year for 60 Minutes.

01:17:03.330 --> 01:17:08.163
And so, yeah, to me,
60 Minutes is sort of,

01:17:11.230 --> 01:17:12.240
a show I've grown up watching

01:17:12.240 --> 01:17:16.350
and yeah, I don't know what.

01:17:16.350 --> 01:17:19.110
I guess I'm doing another
season of the podcast

01:17:19.110 --> 01:17:20.850
and I don't know, I hate,

01:17:20.850 --> 01:17:22.679
I sound like I'm pitching products.

01:17:22.679 --> 01:17:25.620
I don't like, I don't know, I do stuff.

01:17:25.620 --> 01:17:26.970
I don't really know
where my career's going.

01:17:26.970 --> 01:17:28.440
I don't know what comes down the pike.

01:17:28.440 --> 01:17:30.960
But, yeah.

01:17:30.960 --> 01:17:33.090
What I like is being able to just

01:17:33.090 --> 01:17:34.980
to work in all these different realms

01:17:34.980 --> 01:17:36.870
and thankfully, I'm in a place

01:17:36.870 --> 01:17:39.090
where I can do the show on CNN,

01:17:39.090 --> 01:17:40.440
I can do 60 Minutes

01:17:40.440 --> 01:17:43.714
and I was just in Cambodia for 60 Minutes

01:17:43.714 --> 01:17:46.530
for a week on a project,

01:17:46.530 --> 01:17:49.170
an investigative project I'm working on.

01:17:49.170 --> 01:17:50.550
I just interviewed Rick Rubin,

01:17:50.550 --> 01:17:53.820
this really interesting music producer

01:17:53.820 --> 01:17:58.020
who's just about every artist
you know, has worked with him.

01:17:58.020 --> 01:18:00.390
So I like dropping in on people's,

01:18:00.390 --> 01:18:02.670
very different than I would cover on CNN,

01:18:02.670 --> 01:18:06.180
but it's interesting to talk
to creative people and yeah,

01:18:07.017 --> 01:18:08.580
and work with them.

01:18:08.580 --> 01:18:11.940
- On those lines, am I correct
that the Vanderbilt book

01:18:11.940 --> 01:18:16.773
is gonna be becoming a television
mini-series or something?

01:18:16.773 --> 01:18:17.910
- Yeah, I did.

01:18:17.910 --> 01:18:20.444
Yeah, wow, you've done your research.

01:18:20.444 --> 01:18:21.510
(Mary, audience laughing)

01:18:21.510 --> 01:18:26.220
Yeah, I guess Amazon has picked it up for,

01:18:26.220 --> 01:18:27.510
to at least see a pilot

01:18:27.510 --> 01:18:30.780
for a possible like dramatic series on it.

01:18:30.780 --> 01:18:31.980
Yeah, we'll see.

01:18:31.980 --> 01:18:34.770
- So it will be better than
the one that you critiqued

01:18:34.770 --> 01:18:37.440
in your book with Angela Lansbury?

01:18:37.440 --> 01:18:39.630
- Oh, yeah, so when I was a kid,

01:18:39.630 --> 01:18:41.340
so my mom was involved
with this custody battle

01:18:41.340 --> 01:18:42.600
when she was 10-years-old.

01:18:42.600 --> 01:18:43.860
She was taken away.

01:18:43.860 --> 01:18:45.960
It was at the height of the depression.

01:18:45.960 --> 01:18:49.800
My mom was, it's so weird and
complicated to even go into.

01:18:49.800 --> 01:18:52.440
But basically, my mom's mom

01:18:52.440 --> 01:18:55.980
and my mom's great aunt,
Gertrude Vanderbilt Whitney,

01:18:55.980 --> 01:18:57.530
who created the Whitney Museum,

01:18:58.950 --> 01:19:02.073
they sued for custody over
my 10-year-old mother.

01:19:03.000 --> 01:19:04.860
Neither of them really knew my mom,

01:19:04.860 --> 01:19:06.150
including her own mother.

01:19:06.150 --> 01:19:08.070
But they both sued for custody

01:19:08.070 --> 01:19:10.800
and it was called, at the
time, the trial of the century.

01:19:10.800 --> 01:19:15.510
It was these two well-known women

01:19:15.510 --> 01:19:20.130
fighting for custody of this
very rich 10-year-old girl

01:19:20.130 --> 01:19:22.620
who they called little Gloria.

01:19:22.620 --> 01:19:25.620
And the country was divided on it.

01:19:25.620 --> 01:19:27.420
Hundreds of people would show
up to the court every day.

01:19:27.420 --> 01:19:28.653
It went on for months.

01:19:29.880 --> 01:19:32.550
My mom, ultimately, was taken
away from her own mother,

01:19:32.550 --> 01:19:34.140
sent to live with Gertrude
Vanderbilt Whitney

01:19:34.140 --> 01:19:36.000
'cause she was the rich one.

01:19:36.000 --> 01:19:37.650
And my mother was raised by this aunt

01:19:37.650 --> 01:19:38.850
who she didn't even know

01:19:40.047 --> 01:19:41.700
and how do we even get on this topic?

01:19:41.700 --> 01:19:44.220
- Because it's gonna become
a dramatized TV series.

01:19:44.220 --> 01:19:46.350
- Yeah, but I don't know
why I brought up that.

01:19:46.350 --> 01:19:47.183
(Mary, audience laughing)

01:19:47.183 --> 01:19:48.288
Oh no, you brought up the
Angela Lansbury thing.

01:19:48.288 --> 01:19:49.121
- Angela Lansbury.

01:19:49.121 --> 01:19:49.954
- Anyway.
- Yes. Yes.

01:19:49.954 --> 01:19:51.840
- My mom never talked
about this experience

01:19:51.840 --> 01:19:53.280
she had as a kid when I was a kid,

01:19:53.280 --> 01:19:54.930
so I didn't really know anything about it.

01:19:54.930 --> 01:19:57.710
And then one day, some lady wrote a book

01:19:57.710 --> 01:20:00.540
about the custody battle,
Barbara Goldsmith,

01:20:00.540 --> 01:20:04.230
and it became a made for TV miniseries

01:20:04.230 --> 01:20:06.210
with Angela Lansbury.

01:20:06.210 --> 01:20:09.270
And my mom went ballistic

01:20:09.270 --> 01:20:10.800
'cause my mom had never talked about this.

01:20:10.800 --> 01:20:12.510
And it was like deeply personal to her

01:20:12.510 --> 01:20:14.610
and all of a sudden,
it's a made for TV movie.

01:20:14.610 --> 01:20:19.120
So it was really fun the week
that, that TV series was on

01:20:20.040 --> 01:20:23.220
to be in my house 'cause
my mom was very upset.

01:20:23.220 --> 01:20:24.690
So yes, and it's terrible.

01:20:24.690 --> 01:20:27.120
I never saw it until I
wrote the Vanderbilt book

01:20:27.120 --> 01:20:27.953
and I finally saw it

01:20:27.953 --> 01:20:29.760
and yeah, it's really, it's really awful.

01:20:29.760 --> 01:20:30.593
- You're very funny
when you talk about it.

01:20:30.593 --> 01:20:33.840
- It's an amazing story,
but it was done very badly.

01:20:33.840 --> 01:20:36.117
But yeah, so anyway.

01:20:36.117 --> 01:20:39.060
- And it will be much,
much better than that.

01:20:39.060 --> 01:20:40.710
- It will be much, much better than that.

01:20:40.710 --> 01:20:42.935
How many of you are undergraduates here?

01:20:42.935 --> 01:20:43.768
(audience members cheering)

01:20:43.768 --> 01:20:45.000
- Wow.
- Wow.

01:20:45.000 --> 01:20:45.930
- And are you excited?

01:20:45.930 --> 01:20:47.703
Are you optimistic about the world?

01:20:48.692 --> 01:20:49.525
- Yeah!
- Yay!

01:20:49.525 --> 01:20:51.660
(audience cheers, murmurs)

01:20:51.660 --> 01:20:52.650
- I love...

01:20:52.650 --> 01:20:55.020
You said that, you did
that the most sarcastically

01:20:55.020 --> 01:20:56.100
of anybody I've ever,

01:20:56.100 --> 01:20:58.012
you were like, yeah, ah huh.

01:20:58.012 --> 01:20:59.280
(Mary, audience laughing)

01:20:59.280 --> 01:21:00.360
That's such a Yale thing.

01:21:00.360 --> 01:21:01.923
Like yeah, you're optimistic, but yeah,

01:21:01.923 --> 01:21:03.453
it's just sarcastically so.

01:21:04.950 --> 01:21:06.300
I get it.

01:21:06.300 --> 01:21:07.410
- Some things don't change.

01:21:07.410 --> 01:21:09.000
- Some things don't change, yeah.

01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:12.693
Well, yeah, I just think, I just remember,

01:21:14.040 --> 01:21:15.660
I just remember being at Yale

01:21:15.660 --> 01:21:18.690
and being like scared of what was to come

01:21:18.690 --> 01:21:21.780
and wishing I had a plan

01:21:21.780 --> 01:21:24.900
and wishing things were clearer.

01:21:24.900 --> 01:21:28.440
And now, and all the
things that I cared about

01:21:28.440 --> 01:21:29.853
at Yale at the time,

01:21:31.733 --> 01:21:33.720
I didn't really care
so much about classes.

01:21:33.720 --> 01:21:36.300
I was a coxswain on a crew team.

01:21:36.300 --> 01:21:39.690
I thought that, to me that
was, I was obsessed with it.

01:21:39.690 --> 01:21:40.530
Looking back, I'm like,

01:21:40.530 --> 01:21:43.980
I don't know why I was so
obsessed with that, but I was,

01:21:43.980 --> 01:21:47.220
and all the things now
that I look back fondly on

01:21:47.220 --> 01:21:50.220
are not the things I
thought I would remember

01:21:50.220 --> 01:21:51.900
and the things I thought
I would care about.

01:21:51.900 --> 01:21:53.970
All the things now,
it's like little moments

01:21:53.970 --> 01:21:57.510
that I experience with
friends and people on the team

01:21:57.510 --> 01:21:59.370
and just these little moments

01:21:59.370 --> 01:22:01.383
that were meaningless at the time.

01:22:02.400 --> 01:22:03.510
But those are the things,

01:22:03.510 --> 01:22:04.920
like, I was on this crew team

01:22:04.920 --> 01:22:06.570
and I don't remember how we did.

01:22:06.570 --> 01:22:09.810
We apparently did very
well, I've been told.

01:22:09.810 --> 01:22:11.250
But like, I don't remember the races,

01:22:11.250 --> 01:22:12.410
I don't remember where we were

01:22:12.410 --> 01:22:15.570
in the national championships
and stuff like that.

01:22:15.570 --> 01:22:17.970
But I remember like riding on
the bus out to the boathouse.

01:22:17.970 --> 01:22:20.850
I remember the creek on the
stairs in the boathouse.

01:22:20.850 --> 01:22:21.870
Just these little things.

01:22:21.870 --> 01:22:23.910
I remember hanging out in Durfee

01:22:23.910 --> 01:22:27.342
with all my other loser roommates and me.

01:22:27.342 --> 01:22:28.175
(Mary, audience laughing)

01:22:28.175 --> 01:22:31.800
And all that stuff has taken
on a much greater import

01:22:31.800 --> 01:22:33.990
than when I was actually going through it.

01:22:33.990 --> 01:22:36.060
And so, I guess I would just say,

01:22:36.060 --> 01:22:37.560
especially the freshman,

01:22:37.560 --> 01:22:41.340
is just to enjoy the
experience you're having

01:22:41.340 --> 01:22:43.980
'cause what you're gonna
remember of it is so different

01:22:43.980 --> 01:22:46.770
than what you think you're
gonna remember of it.

01:22:46.770 --> 01:22:48.750
And you're gonna hear like,

01:22:48.750 --> 01:22:50.520
and it won't have any reality for you

01:22:50.520 --> 01:22:52.500
until you're an old person like me

01:22:52.500 --> 01:22:54.060
and coming back and you'll be like,

01:22:54.060 --> 01:22:56.250
oh, I don't remember this,

01:22:56.250 --> 01:22:58.410
but I remember the ice
cream at Durfee Hall

01:22:58.410 --> 01:22:59.243
or whatever it was.

01:22:59.243 --> 01:23:01.798
So anyway, that's it.

01:23:01.798 --> 01:23:03.383
- All right.
- That's all I got.

01:23:03.383 --> 01:23:05.893
- All right, please join me
in thanking Anderson Cooper.

01:23:05.893 --> 01:23:10.893
- Thank you.
(audience clapping)

